{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014kZv2f2yeqrTHRoFe4Am23","type":"tool_result","content":"## 10. The search from the other end, and the meet in the band: dead, by asymmetry\n\n*(2026-11-04, same day; `learning/two_ended.py`, 14 tests in\n`tests/test_two_ended.py`; `reports/scratch/checkpoint/two_ended/`.)*\n\n§9 proposed a search from the other end, or a meet in the band with the two\nfrontiers stored, because the search's states peak before the band its\ncertificate needs. Measured rule-free on the 64 instances at 30–50, the\nidea dies on a one-line fact about the state lattice.\n\n**The lattice.** A closing order is feasible for \"MOSP ≤ k\" exactly when\nevery prefix `S` has `|N(S) ∖ S| ≤ k − 1` (closing `c` from `S` costs\n`|N[S ∪ c] ∖ S| = |N(S ∪ c) ∖ (S ∪ c)| + 1`, `satisfiability/customer_search.py`).\nSo the decision is reachability from `∅` to `V` inside\n`F_k = {S : |N(S) ∖ S| ≤ k − 1}` by single additions. Reversing the\nproduction sequence maps closing orders to closing orders of the same graph\n(the opening order, reversed), so the \"reversed\" search is the same search.\nThe genuinely different direction builds the order from its **end**: start\nat `S = V`, remove the customer closed last, and require the prefix that\nremains to be in `F_k`. Forward frontier `R_f(i)`: the sets of size `i`\nreachable from `∅`; backward frontier `R_b(i)`: those from which `V` is\nreachable. Both enumerated exactly (bitmask layers; the backward one keeps\na per-vertex count of neighbours still inside, updated for all candidates\nat once), verified against a subset DP on 485 (graph, k) cases.\n\n**The asymmetry.** `|N(S) ∖ S| ≤ |V ∖ S|`: a set missing at most `k − 1`\nvertices is feasible whatever it is, and so is every set between it and\n`V`. Hence `R_b(n − j) = C(n, j)` for every `j ≤ k − 1`, before any\nconstraint bites: the last `k − 1` customers closed are unconstrained, and\nthe backward search must choose them blind. At 30 customers and `k = 11`\nthe sixth backward layer has 2,035,800 states; the enumeration hit its\n3-million cap on 61 of 64 instances a median of five levels below `V`. At\n125 customers and `k = 24` the first constrained backward layer is\n`C(125, 23) ≈ 10²⁶` states. The forward direction is constrained from the\nfirst move: closing a customer opens all its neighbours. No decision\ndiagram stores a binomial frontier, and a meet in the band needs the\nbackward frontier at the band, which is larger still.\n\n**The forward frontier, calibrated.** Forward profiles on all 64 (none\ncensored), with the search's own counts from §7 beside them:\n\n| n | instances | wall `i_f / n` | peak layer `/ n` | rule-free states, median (max) | free-closed states | search nodes | rule-free states / search nodes | free-closed / search's distinct sets | states in the 2nd half of the climb |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 30 | 1 | 0.37 | 0.07 | 251 | 66 | 80 | 3.14 | 1.02 | 22% |\n| 40 | 19 | 0.48 | 0.12 | 5,537 (80,815) | 1,503 | 1,979 | 2.54 | 1.66 | 13% |\n| 50 | 44 | 0.46 | 0.12 | 42,346 (281,270) | 11,022 | 15,443 | 1.86 | 1.58 | 8% |\n\n(Sizes here are closed-customer counts `|S|`; §7's `0.42 n` peak is in\nprefix units `|N[S]|`, and the band of §5 is in those units too.) Three\nthings follow. The wall sits at `0.46 n` closed customers (IQR 0.40–0.54),\nthe frontier peaks at `0.12 n`, and 90% of the reachable states lie in the\nfirst half of the climb: the search's cost is the breadth of *beginnings*,\nnot the approach to the wall. The rule-free reachable lattice is only\n2.3× the search's node count (IQR 1.4–3.2), and its free-closed part 1.6×\nthe search's distinct sets: **the search already visits about as many\nstates as the lattice holds**, so no traversal order, store or diagram over\nthe same lattice can save more than a small factor. And the lattice is what\nit is: at 125 on the ridge, `10¹¹` nodes is its size, not the search's\ninefficiency. The only way past it is a certificate that does not enumerate\nprefixes, which is the SAT route of §3–§6, and that returns at 75 on the\ndense side and not on the ridge.\n\nThe proposal in §9 is withdrawn. Its measured reason stood — the states do\nsit before the band — but the conclusion drawn from it was wrong: those\nstates are not wasted work before the obstruction, they *are* the\nobstruction's size as a reachability problem.\n\n## 9. Verdict and what follows\n\n- **As a bound family: closed.** A relaxation with few *fixed* checkpoints\n  gains a quarter of a stack on the residual. Compact certificates exist at\n  gap 1 (five consecutive sizes at `0.5–0.7 n`, §5), but finding them took\n  exact segment checks, and at 75 the relaxation's own SAT calls censor on\n  the ridge before the number of checkpoints matters (§4, §6). The\n  obstruction is a band of consecutive nested levels: the exact problem,\n  localised. This was the last argument aimed at the property that killed\n  the single-prefix bounds, and it does not give a floor.\n- **Reusable, and the result worth keeping**: the nested chain as an exact\n  encoding with a DRAT proof object. At 41–50 customers it refutes twice\n  what the position encoding does in the same budget and every instance\n  with many patterns; at 75 it refutes every dense instance in under an\n  hour (§6, §8). A Lean statement of its soundness is one lemma (a layout's\n  prefixes are nested and bounded) and is proposed if it is adopted for\n  `learning.proofs`.\n- **The solver idea this suggested is dead** (§10, tested the same day):\n  a search from the other end must choose the last `k − 1` customers blind\n  (`R_b(n − j) = C(n, j)`), a meet in the band needs that frontier, and the\n  forward search already visits about as many states as the reachable\n  lattice holds (rule-free states 2.3× its nodes). The `10¹¹` nodes at 125\n  are the lattice's size.\n- **Not done**: 100–125 customers, where nothing here reaches; the\n  general CEGAR loop of §4 is sound and general (vertex separation with\n  exact segment checks) but loses to one whole-chain call at 75.\n======\n\"\"\"Two-ended search: the state lattice of a refutation, forward and backward.\n\nA closing order `c_1 … c_n` of the customers is feasible for \"MOSP ≤ k\"\nexactly when every prefix `S_t = {c_1 … c_t}` satisfies `|N(S_t) ∖ S_t| ≤ k − 1`\n(`satisfiability/customer_search.py`: closing `c` from `S` costs\n`|N[S ∪ c] ∖ S| = |N(S ∪ c) ∖ (S ∪ c)| + 1`). So \"MOSP ≤ k\" is reachability\nfrom `∅` to `V` inside the family `F_k = {S : |N(S) ∖ S| ≤ k − 1}` by single\nadditions. Reversing the production sequence maps closing orders to closing\norders of the same graph (the opening order reversed), so it gives the same\nsearch; the genuinely different direction is building the order from its\n**end**: states are suffixes `T = V ∖ S`, walked from `V` by single removals\nunder the same membership test, `|∂_in(T)| ≤ k − 1`.\n\nThis module enumerates both frontiers exactly and rule-free: `R_f(i)`, the\nsets of size `i` reachable from `∅` inside `F_k`, and `R_b(i)`, those from\nwhich `V` is reachable. A refutation at `k` is `R_f(i) = ∅` for some `i`\n(the forward wall `i_f`), equivalently `R_b(i) = ∅` for some `i` (the\nbackward wall `i_b`), equivalently `R_f(i) ∩ R_b(i) = ∅` at every `i`. The\nforward search's work is `Σ_{i < i_f} |R_f(i)|`, the backward's\n`Σ_{i > i_b} |R_b(i)|`, and a meet at level `i` costs\n`Σ_{j ≤ i} |R_f(j)| + Σ_{j ≥ i} |R_b(j)|`. The checkpoint anatomy\n(`reports/checkpoint_relaxation.md` §5, §7) says the forward search's states\npeak at `0.42 n` while the obstruction sits at `0.5–0.7 n`; this measures\nwhether the other end, or a meet, pays — in states, which is what a search\nwith a frontier store would hold.\n\nFree moves (`N[v] ⊆ N[S]`) are a dominance the forward search applies; the\ncounts here are without any rule on either side, so the two directions are\ncompared like with like. `forward_closed(i)` counts the forward states with\nno free move available, the ones the rule-based search can actually visit.\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport csv\nimport json\nimport os\nimport time\nfrom multiprocessing import Pool\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\n\nfrom learning.checkpoint_bound import OUT, pilot_population\nfrom learning.obstruction_ledger import adjacency_from_matrix\n\n\ndef forward_layers(adj: list[int], k: int, cap: int) -> tuple[list[int], list[int], bool]:\n    \"\"\"`|R_f(i)|` for i = 0..n (stops at the first empty layer), the count of\n    free-closed states per layer, and whether a layer exceeded `cap`.\"\"\"\n    n = len(adj)\n    full = (1 << n) - 1\n    bound = k - 1\n    closed_nb = [adj[v] | (1 << v) for v in range(n)]\n    layer: dict[int, int] = {0: 0}       # S -> N[S]\n    sizes, closed_counts = [1], [1]\n    for i in range(n):\n        nxt: dict[int, int] = {}\n        for S, NS in layer.items():\n            bits = full & ~S\n            while bits:\n                bit = bits & -bits\n                bits ^= bit\n                v = bit.bit_length() - 1\n                S2 = S | bit\n                NS2 = NS | closed_nb[v]\n                if (NS2 & ~S2).bit_count() <= bound and S2 not in nxt:\n                    nxt[S2] = NS2\n                    if len(nxt) > cap:\n                        return sizes, closed_counts, True\n        if not nxt:\n            sizes.append(0)\n            closed_counts.append(0)\n            return sizes, closed_counts, False\n        free_closed = 0\n        for S, NS in nxt.items():\n            ok = True\n            bits = full & ~S\n            while bits:\n=====\n# loop0014 — pathwidth after ETH: items\n\nOpenAI's family 378 (*The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False*, 2026-10-25,\nLean-verified against a comparator statement) gives a deterministic\n`#k`-SAT algorithm subexponential in the **number of variables**. Pathwidth\nis a permutation problem whose natural CNF encodings use `Θ(n²)` variables,\nso the theorem says nothing about it directly, and every \"no `2^{o(n)}`\nunless ETH fails\" statement about width parameters is void if the theorem\nstands. The plan, `reports/subexp_pathwidth_plan.md`, fixes the premise as a\nnamed `Prop` never asserted (§0.4.1), linearity in vertices as the only\ntransfer (§0.4.2), classes before the general case (§0.4.3), no compute on\ninstances (§0.4.4), four subagents per session at most (§0.4.5), and brute\nforce to 7 vertices before any statement (§0.4.6). Kill criteria are in\n§0.6; item 01 can kill the loop.\n\nThe report is `reports/subexp_pathwidth.md`; scratch under\n`reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/<item>/`; Lean under\n`lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/` (imported by\n`lean/MOSPFormalization.lean`).\n\n- [ ] **01 The audit and the kill check: exact bases, ETH-conditional lower bounds, and what the counting method needs from a problem.**\n  `pdftotext` the ETH paper once; read Sections 1, 2 (technique overview),\n  6 (the reduction of `#k`-SAT to counting common zeros) and Appendix D\n  (tensor contraction), and skim 3–5 for the objects (shared linear\n  summaries, low-degree replacement forms, sparse polynomial systems).\n  Write `reports/subexp_pathwidth.md` §1 with: (a) the best published exact\n  algorithms for pathwidth / vertex separation, treewidth, bandwidth and\n  cutwidth with their bases and citations (held papers under `literature/`\n  first; Suchan & Villanger, Kitsunai et al., Bodlaender–Fomin–Koster–\n  Kratsch–Thilikos, Fomin & Villanger, Cygan & Pilipczuk for bandwidth);\n  (b) every ETH- or SETH-conditional lower bound on those parameters we can\n  find in held papers or in the citation graph, each with the reduction it\n  rests on and the reduction's vertex blow-up; (c) **the two-page account\n  of what the counting method needs from a problem, in our words**: the\n  input form (variables per object, clause width, sparsity), where the\n  `2^{n/√log n}` comes from, what breaks if the constraint width is\n  unbounded, and whether the method is a counting method only or decides\n  structured existence too. Then the kill check of plan §0.6: stop the\n  loop (mark 02–09 `[!]`, leave 10–11) if a `2^{o(n)}` exact pathwidth\n  algorithm is on record or the method demonstrably cannot apply to any\n  NP-hard width problem. One subagent at most. Ends with \"what this does\n  not claim\".\n\n- [ ] **02 The formal frame: `SubexpCount`, an algorithm-with-time-bound notion, and `PathwidthSubexp`.**\n  In `lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/Frame.lean`: decide, and\n  record in the report, whether to copy OpenAI's `PrimitiveMachine` model\n  (`lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean`, 220 lines over\n  Mathlib's `Turing.TM0`; Apache-2.0, verbatim with attribution in the file\n  header and a `literature/MISSING.md` entry) or to use an abstract notion\n  (a decidable predicate with a time function, `∃ T : ℕ → ℕ, (∀ ε > 0,\n  eventually T n ≤ 2^(ε n)) ∧ …`). Define `SubexpCount (k : ℕ) : Prop`\n  restating `exact_count_and_sat` (never proved, never an axiom: a `def`\n  of a `Prop`), `PathwidthSubexp : Prop` (\"some machine decides `pw G ≤ k`\n  for every `n`-vertex `G` and `k` in time `2^{o(n)} · poly`\"), and the\n  class-restricted `PathwidthSubexpOn (C : SimpleGraph → Prop)`. Prove the\n  two trivial lemmas (monotonicity in the class; `PathwidthSubexp ↔\n  PathwidthSubexpOn (fun _ => True)`). `lake build`; axiom audit; no\n  `sorry`.\n\n- [ ] **03 The reduction catalogue: every reduction into pathwidth or vertex separation on record, with its blow-up; the best linear `X`.**\n=====\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Regression gate for loop0014 (pathwidth after ETH).\n\nPasses only if all five hold:\n1. `lake build` in lean/ succeeds;\n2. every `.lean` file under lean/MOSPFormalization/ (top level, Complex/ and\n   Search/) is imported by lean/MOSPFormalization.lean, so the build really\n   checks it;\n3. the number of `sorry` in Lean *code* (comments and docstrings stripped) is\n   at most the baseline (0: the development has none since Appendix E) plus\n   the entries in allowed_sorries.txt, and no `axiom` declaration exists;\n4. `python -m pytest tests/ -q -x` passes, and so does `pytest` in\n   pathwidth_solver/;\n5. the paper builds with no undefined references (`make -C paper1/latex check`).\n\n    python3 Ralph_Loops/loop0014/gate.py\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport os\nimport re\nimport signal\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\nREPO = HERE.parents[1]\nLEAN = REPO / \"lean\"\nBASELINE_SORRIES = 0\nALLOWED = HERE / \"allowed_sorries.txt\"\nPAPER = REPO / \"paper1\" / \"latex\"\n\n\ndef strip_comments(src: str) -> str:\n    out, i, depth = [], 0, 0\n    while i < len(src):\n        if src.startswith(\"/-\", i):\n            depth += 1; i += 2; continue\n        if depth and src.startswith(\"-/\", i):\n            depth -= 1; i += 2; continue\n        if depth:\n            i += 1; continue\n        if src.startswith(\"--\", i):\n            j = src.find(\"\\n\", i); i = len(src) if j < 0 else j; continue\n        if src[i] == '\"':\n            j = i + 1\n            while j < len(src) and src[j] != '\"':\n                j += 2 if src[j] == \"\\\\\" else 1\n            i = j + 1; continue\n        out.append(src[i]); i += 1\n    return \"\".join(out)\n\n\ndef _run(cmd: list[str], cwd: Path, timeout: float) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:\n    \"\"\"`subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, timeout=timeout)` with two\n    differences, both from the loop0014 item 01 gate hang (2026-11-02): the\n    command runs in its own session, so that on timeout its whole process group\n    is killed (`subprocess.run` kills only the direct child, and the forked\n    pytest worker that had hung outlived the gate by an hour), and the output\n    produced before the timeout travels with the exception, so that `main` can\n    print which test was running.\"\"\"\n    proc = subprocess.Popen(cmd, cwd=cwd, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,\n                            text=True, errors=\"replace\", start_new_session=True)\n    try:\n        out, _ = proc.communicate(timeout=timeout)\n    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:\n        os.killpg(proc.pid, signal.SIGKILL)\n        out, _ = proc.communicate()\n        raise subprocess.TimeoutExpired(cmd, timeout, output=out)\n    return subprocess.CompletedProcess(cmd, proc.returncode, out, \"\")\n\n\n# A test that runs this long has every thread's stack dumped to stderr (pytest's\n# faulthandler plugin); the test goes on, and the dump names it if the gate\n# then times out. The suite takes about 5 min in all.\nFAULTHANDLER = \"faulthandler_timeout=900\"\n\n\ndef _main() -> int:","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9be64548-7b53-4f99-81aa-4ed97f3b0de3","uuid":"c77a1b08-6809-4db8-b321-b05df17ac65a","timestamp":"2026-11-04T18:07:52.254Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"## 10. The search from the other end, and the meet in the band: dead, by asymmetry\n\n*(2026-11-04, same day; `learning/two_ended.py`, 14 tests in\n`tests/test_two_ended.py`; `reports/scratch/checkpoint/two_ended/`.)*\n\n§9 proposed a search from the other end, or a meet in the band with the two\nfrontiers stored, because the search's states peak before the band its\ncertificate needs. Measured rule-free on the 64 instances at 30–50, the\nidea dies on a one-line fact about the state lattice.\n\n**The lattice.** A closing order is feasible for \"MOSP ≤ k\" exactly when\nevery prefix `S` has `|N(S) ∖ S| ≤ k − 1` (closing `c` from `S` costs\n`|N[S ∪ c] ∖ S| = |N(S ∪ c) ∖ (S ∪ c)| + 1`, `satisfiability/customer_search.py`).\nSo the decision is reachability from `∅` to `V` inside\n`F_k = {S : |N(S) ∖ S| ≤ k − 1}` by single additions. Reversing the\nproduction sequence maps closing orders to closing orders of the same graph\n(the opening order, reversed), so the \"reversed\" search is the same search.\nThe genuinely different direction builds the order from its **end**: start\nat `S = V`, remove the customer closed last, and require the prefix that\nremains to be in `F_k`. Forward frontier `R_f(i)`: the sets of size `i`\nreachable from `∅`; backward frontier `R_b(i)`: those from which `V` is\nreachable. Both enumerated exactly (bitmask layers; the backward one keeps\na per-vertex count of neighbours still inside, updated for all candidates\nat once), verified against a subset DP on 485 (graph, k) cases.\n\n**The asymmetry.** `|N(S) ∖ S| ≤ |V ∖ S|`: a set missing at most `k − 1`\nvertices is feasible whatever it is, and so is every set between it and\n`V`. Hence `R_b(n − j) = C(n, j)` for every `j ≤ k − 1`, before any\nconstraint bites: the last `k − 1` customers closed are unconstrained, and\nthe backward search must choose them blind. At 30 customers and `k = 11`\nthe sixth backward layer has 2,035,800 states; the enumeration hit its\n3-million cap on 61 of 64 instances a median of five levels below `V`. At\n125 customers and `k = 24` the first constrained backward layer is\n`C(125, 23) ≈ 10²⁶` states. The forward direction is constrained from the\nfirst move: closing a customer opens all its neighbours. No decision\ndiagram stores a binomial frontier, and a meet in the band needs the\nbackward frontier at the band, which is larger still.\n\n**The forward frontier, calibrated.** Forward profiles on all 64 (none\ncensored), with the search's own counts from §7 beside them:\n\n| n | instances | wall `i_f / n` | peak layer `/ n` | rule-free states, median (max) | free-closed states | search nodes | rule-free states / search nodes | free-closed / search's distinct sets | states in the 2nd half of the climb |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 30 | 1 | 0.37 | 0.07 | 251 | 66 | 80 | 3.14 | 1.02 | 22% |\n| 40 | 19 | 0.48 | 0.12 | 5,537 (80,815) | 1,503 | 1,979 | 2.54 | 1.66 | 13% |\n| 50 | 44 | 0.46 | 0.12 | 42,346 (281,270) | 11,022 | 15,443 | 1.86 | 1.58 | 8% |\n\n(Sizes here are closed-customer counts `|S|`; §7's `0.42 n` peak is in\nprefix units `|N[S]|`, and the band of §5 is in those units too.) Three\nthings follow. The wall sits at `0.46 n` closed customers (IQR 0.40–0.54),\nthe frontier peaks at `0.12 n`, and 90% of the reachable states lie in the\nfirst half of the climb: the search's cost is the breadth of *beginnings*,\nnot the approach to the wall. The rule-free reachable lattice is only\n2.3× the search's node count (IQR 1.4–3.2), and its free-closed part 1.6×\nthe search's distinct sets: **the search already visits about as many\nstates as the lattice holds**, so no traversal order, store or diagram over\nthe same lattice can save more than a small factor. And the lattice is what\nit is: at 125 on the ridge, `10¹¹` nodes is its size, not the search's\ninefficiency. The only way past it is a certificate that does not enumerate\nprefixes, which is the SAT route of §3–§6, and that returns at 75 on the\ndense side and not on the ridge.\n\nThe proposal in §9 is withdrawn. Its measured reason stood — the states do\nsit before the band — but the conclusion drawn from it was wrong: those\nstates are not wasted work before the obstruction, they *are* the\nobstruction's size as a reachability problem.\n\n## 9. Verdict and what follows\n\n- **As a bound family: closed.** A relaxation with few *fixed* checkpoints\n  gains a quarter of a stack on the residual. Compact certificates exist at\n  gap 1 (five consecutive sizes at `0.5–0.7 n`, §5), but finding them took\n  exact segment checks, and at 75 the relaxation's own SAT calls censor on\n  the ridge before the number of checkpoints matters (§4, §6). The\n  obstruction is a band of consecutive nested levels: the exact problem,\n  localised. This was the last argument aimed at the property that killed\n  the single-prefix bounds, and it does not give a floor.\n- **Reusable, and the result worth keeping**: the nested chain as an exact\n  encoding with a DRAT proof object. At 41–50 customers it refutes twice\n  what the position encoding does in the same budget and every instance\n  with many patterns; at 75 it refutes every dense instance in under an\n  hour (§6, §8). A Lean statement of its soundness is one lemma (a layout's\n  prefixes are nested and bounded) and is proposed if it is adopted for\n  `learning.proofs`.\n- **The solver idea this suggested is dead** (§10, tested the same day):\n  a search from the other end must choose the last `k − 1` customers blind\n  (`R_b(n − j) = C(n, j)`), a meet in the band needs that frontier, and the\n  forward search already visits about as many states as the reachable\n  lattice holds (rule-free states 2.3× its nodes). The `10¹¹` nodes at 125\n  are the lattice's size.\n- **Not done**: 100–125 customers, where nothing here reaches; the\n  general CEGAR loop of §4 is sound and general (vertex separation with\n  exact segment checks) but loses to one whole-chain call at 75.\n======\n\"\"\"Two-ended search: the state lattice of a refutation, forward and backward.\n\nA closing order `c_1 … c_n` of the customers is feasible for \"MOSP ≤ k\"\nexactly when every prefix `S_t = {c_1 … c_t}` satisfies `|N(S_t) ∖ S_t| ≤ k − 1`\n(`satisfiability/customer_search.py`: closing `c` from `S` costs\n`|N[S ∪ c] ∖ S| = |N(S ∪ c) ∖ (S ∪ c)| + 1`). So \"MOSP ≤ k\" is reachability\nfrom `∅` to `V` inside the family `F_k = {S : |N(S) ∖ S| ≤ k − 1}` by single\nadditions. Reversing the production sequence maps closing orders to closing\norders of the same graph (the opening order reversed), so it gives the same\nsearch; the genuinely different direction is building the order from its\n**end**: states are suffixes `T = V ∖ S`, walked from `V` by single removals\nunder the same membership test, `|∂_in(T)| ≤ k − 1`.\n\nThis module enumerates both frontiers exactly and rule-free: `R_f(i)`, the\nsets of size `i` reachable from `∅` inside `F_k`, and `R_b(i)`, those from\nwhich `V` is reachable. A refutation at `k` is `R_f(i) = ∅` for some `i`\n(the forward wall `i_f`), equivalently `R_b(i) = ∅` for some `i` (the\nbackward wall `i_b`), equivalently `R_f(i) ∩ R_b(i) = ∅` at every `i`. The\nforward search's work is `Σ_{i < i_f} |R_f(i)|`, the backward's\n`Σ_{i > i_b} |R_b(i)|`, and a meet at level `i` costs\n`Σ_{j ≤ i} |R_f(j)| + Σ_{j ≥ i} |R_b(j)|`. The checkpoint anatomy\n(`reports/checkpoint_relaxation.md` §5, §7) says the forward search's states\npeak at `0.42 n` while the obstruction sits at `0.5–0.7 n`; this measures\nwhether the other end, or a meet, pays — in states, which is what a search\nwith a frontier store would hold.\n\nFree moves (`N[v] ⊆ N[S]`) are a dominance the forward search applies; the\ncounts here are without any rule on either side, so the two directions are\ncompared like with like. `forward_closed(i)` counts the forward states with\nno free move available, the ones the rule-based search can actually visit.\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport csv\nimport json\nimport os\nimport time\nfrom multiprocessing import Pool\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\n\nfrom learning.checkpoint_bound import OUT, pilot_population\nfrom learning.obstruction_ledger import adjacency_from_matrix\n\n\ndef forward_layers(adj: list[int], k: int, cap: int) -> tuple[list[int], list[int], bool]:\n    \"\"\"`|R_f(i)|` for i = 0..n (stops at the first empty layer), the count of\n    free-closed states per layer, and whether a layer exceeded `cap`.\"\"\"\n    n = len(adj)\n    full = (1 << n) - 1\n    bound = k - 1\n    closed_nb = [adj[v] | (1 << v) for v in range(n)]\n    layer: dict[int, int] = {0: 0}       # S -> N[S]\n    sizes, closed_counts = [1], [1]\n    for i in range(n):\n        nxt: dict[int, int] = {}\n        for S, NS in layer.items():\n            bits = full & ~S\n            while bits:\n                bit = bits & -bits\n                