teract only through a small collection\n420\tof shared linear summaries. Once the values of those summaries are fixed, we can again sum over\n421\teach group separately.\n422\t\f                             THE EXPONENTIAL TIME HYPOTHESIS IS FALSE                                       9\n423\t\n424\t\n425\t2.3. A polynomial family with easier evaluation points. We introduce a parameter T . At\n426\tT = 0, the replacements must restore the original clause inputs. At selected nonzero points\n427\tT = 1, . . . , Q, they should let us separate the groups by fixing shared linear summaries. Here Q\n428\tis the number of selected points, and R will bound the degree of the replacement coefficients. We\n429\tchoose both parameters later.\n430\t   Write X ⃗ = (X1 , . . . , Xn ) for the vector of formal variables. For each occurrence v ∈ Ie , use the\n431\tpolynomial coefficient vector P⃗e,v (T ) = (pe,v,1 (T ), . . . , pe,v,n (T )) to replace Xv in clause e by the\n432\tscalar inner product\n433\t                                                          n\n434\t                                                          X\n435\t                                         ⟨P⃗e,v (T ), ⃗\n436\t                                                     X⟩ =    pe,v,i (T )Xi .\n437\t                                                         i=1\n438\tWe call this expression the replacement linear form for the occurrence of variable v in clause e. It\n439\treplaces the input Xv in the clause polynomial Φe . We require"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9be64548-7b53-4f99-81aa-4ed97f3b0de3","uuid":"762a3742-d25a-45f1-b01b-04c35675cd11","timestamp":"2026-11-04T18:08:03.152Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/01_audit/eth.txt","content":"  4. Constructing low-degree replacement forms                                                               26\n  5. Counting common zeros of sparse polynomial equations                                                    35\n  6. Reducing #k-SAT to counting common zeros                                                                39\n  Appendix A. Partitioning the satisfying assignments                                                        44\n  Appendix B. Exact arithmetic and space                                                                     47\n  Appendix C. Additional Consequences of Refuting ETH                                                        51\n  Appendix D. A sharper bound for exact counting                                                             53\n  References                                                                                                 64\n\n\n\n                                               1. Introduction\n   For every fixed k ⩾ 3, k-SAT is the canonical NP-complete problem [Coo71], and its exponential-\ntime complexity underlies the Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH) and the Strong Exponential\nTime Hypothesis (SETH) [IP01, IPZ01]. In this paper, we refute ETH by giving, for every fixed\nk ⩾ 2, a deterministic algorithm that counts the satisfying assignments of a k-CNF in subexponential\ntime and polynomial space.\n   Write the input k-CNF on n declared variables with m input clauses as\n                                                m\n                                                ^ \u0002                                         \u0003\n                         F (x1 , . . . , xn ) =    (xie,1 ⊕ be,1 ) ∨ · · · ∨ (xie,k ⊕ be,k ) ,\n                                              e=1\n\nwhere ie,j ∈ {1, . . . , n} and be,j ∈ {0, 1} are the variable index and negation bit of the jth literal in\nclause e, and ⊕ denotes addition modulo two. Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than k\nliterals. The indices ie,j and bits be,j are fixed by F ; only x1 , . . . , xn vary over assignments. Our aim\nis to compute the quantity\n                                      #F = {x ∈ {0, 1}n : F (x) = 1} .                                   (1.1)\nTesting #F > 0 also decides satisfiability. We keep k fixed as n grows. Throughout, log denotes the\nnatural logarithm.\n\n  Date: 17 October 2026, revised 25 October 2026.\n                                                          1\n\f2                                                  OPENAI\n\n\nTheorem 1.1. For each fixed k ⩾ 2, there exists a deterministic algorithm that, given a k-CNF F\non n ⩾ 20 declared variables with m clauses, computes #F as defined in (1.1) in\n                                                                  √\n                                    (n + m)Ok (1) 2Ok (n log log n/ log n)\nbit operations and (n + m)Ok (1) bits of space. For each fixed D > 0, its running time on inputs with\nm ⩽ Dn is                                                     √\n                                     (n + m)Ok,D (1) 2Ok,D (n/ log n) .\n    The rational interpolation argument in Section 6.4 gives the weaker exponent Ok (ρ(n)), where\n                                           n(log log n)2         n\n                                  ρ(n) =      √          =         1       .\n                                                log n      (log n) 2 +o(1)\nAppendix D proves both bounds in Theorem 1.1 by tensor contraction.\n   When #F > 0, the standard self-reduction finds a satisfying assignment by fixing one variable at\na time and checking that the remaining count is positive. Choosing each bit according to the two\nconditional counts instead gives an exactly uniform random satisfying assignment, with polyk (n, m)\nexpected overhead [JVV86, Theorem 3.3(1)].\n   The theorem also yields arbitrarily accurate subexponential-time approximation algorithms for\nMax-k-SAT. The Unique Games Theorem [Ope26, Section 8.1] implies NP-hardness bounds for\nMax-k-SAT that match, up to arbitrarily small error in the approximation ratio, the guarantees of\nthe semidefinite-programming algorithm of [Rag08]. Write OPT(F ) for the maximum number of\nclauses of F simultaneously satisfied by an assignment.\nCorollary 1.2. For every fixed k ⩾ 2 and ε > 0, there exists a randomized algorithm that, given a\nk-CNF F on n ⩾ 20 variables with m clauses, finds an assignment satisfying at least OPT(F ) − εm\nclauses with probability 1 − 2−Ω(n) , in\n                                        (n + m)Ok,ε (1) 2Ok,ε (ρ(n))\ntime and (n + m)Ok,ε (1) space.\nProof. We may assume m > 0. Let f (x) be the fraction of clauses satisfied by x, and let fb(x) be\nthat fraction in a sample of O(n/ε2 ) clauses chosen independently and uniformly with replacement.\nHoeffding’s inequality and a union bound over all 2n assignments give, with probability 1 − 2−Ω(n) ,\n                           |fb(x) − f (x)| ⩽ ε/2    for every x ∈ {0, 1}n .\nThus, if x                       x) ⩾ maxx f (x) − ε. Given an integer threshold b, a Boolean circuit\n         b maximizes fb, then f (b\nwith at most two inputs per gate and size Ok,ε (n) tests whether an assignment satisfies at least b\nsampled clauses. The standard circuit-to-3-CNF reduction [Bar23, Lemmas 15.9–15.10] produces\na formula with Ok,ε (n) variables and clauses that is satisfiable exactly when this threshold can be\nattained. Theorem 1.1, binary search on the threshold, and self-reduction then find an exact sample\noptimizer within the stated bounds. Truncating rejection sampling after O(n + log(n/ε2 )) trials\nper sample gives worst-case poly(n, m, ε−1 ) sampling time and adds at most 2−Ω(n) to the failure\nprobability.                                                                                       □\n   Note that runtime bounds of the form 2o(n) do not transfer under general polynomial-time\n              o(1)\nreductions (2n     would be needed for this), so our result does not imply that general NP problems\nadmit subexponential time algorithms. However we do obtain subexponential time algorithms for\ncounting graph colorings and perfect matchings.\nCorollary 1.3. On simple graphs with v vertices and e edges, the number of independent sets and,\nfor each fixed q ⩾ 2, the number of proper q-colorings can be computed deterministically in 2o(v) time\nand polyq (v, e) space. The number of perfect matchings can be computed deterministically in 2o(v+e)\ntime and poly(v, e) space.\n\f                           THE EXPONENTIAL TIME HYPOTHESIS IS FALSE                                 3\n\n\nProof. For G = (V, E), introduce a Boolean variable xu for each u ∈ V . Satisfying assignments of\n                                        ^\n                                             (¬xu ∨ ¬xw )\n                                        {u,w}∈E\n\ncorrespond bijectively to the independent sets {u ∈ V : xu = 1}. For proper q-colorings, use one\nBoolean variable per vertex and color, require exactly one color at each vertex, and forbid equal\ncolors at adjacent vertices. This gives a counting-preserving q-CNF encoding with qv variables and\nOq (v + e) clauses.\n   For perfect matchings, use one Boolean variable per edge and a linear-size circuit checking that\nexactly one selected edge meets each vertex. The standard circuit-to-3-CNF reduction [Bar23,\nLemmas 15.9–15.10] gives O(v + e) variables and clauses. The gate variables have unique values\nonce the edge bits are fixed, so this encoding also preserves the count. Theorem 1.1 gives the stated\ntime and space bounds.                                                                             □\n  The fixed-density case of Theorem 1.1 gives a circuit lower bound for ENP , the class of languages\ndecidable in deterministic time 2O(n) with an NP oracle.\nCorollary 1.4. Let CL (n) be the minimum number of noninput gates in a circuit computing L on\n{0, 1}n , allowing arbitrary Boolean gates of fan-in at most two and unrestricted fan-out. There exist\na language L ∈ ENP and a constant c > 0 such that\n                                         p\n                             CL (n) > c n log n      for infinitely many n.                      (1.2)\n  Appendix C.1 proves the corollary. Appendix C.2 gives algorithms for inversion and exact preimage\ncounting of Boolean maps with constant output locality.\n1.1. Related work. We first discuss earlier SAT and counting algorithms and the complexity\nhypotheses contradicted by Theorem 1.1. We then compare our approach with earlier algebraic\nalgorithms and identify the existing constructions used in our proof, whose roles are developed in\nthe roadmap in Section 2.\n1.1.1. SAT algorithms and the exponential-time hypotheses. Schöning’s local-search algorithm and\nPPSZ are landmarks in the development of exponential-time algorithms for k-SAT [Sch99, PPSZ05].\nSchöning’s randomized algorithm starts from a random assignment and repeatedly flips a uniformly\nchosen variable in an unsatisfied clause. Its bounded-error decision time is\n                                       (n + m)O(1) (2 − 2/k)n\nfor each fixed k ⩾ 3 [Sch99, Section 2]. In particular, this gives (n + m)O(1) (4/3)n time for 3-SAT.\nThe PPSZ algorithm [PPSZ05] uses a different mechanism: bounded resolution derives additional\nclauses, after which variables are assigned in a random order, taking forced values when available and\nguessing otherwise. For k = 3, 4, its original analysis gave stronger bounds for uniquely satisfiable\nformulas than for general ones. The unique-SAT guarantees were later extended to general 3-SAT\nand 4-SAT; for 3-SAT this gave a randomized O(1.308n ) bound [Her14]. Hansen, Kaplan, Zamir, and\nZwick improved the PPSZ bound for every fixed k ⩾ 3 by biasing the algorithm’s guesses [HKZZ19].\nScheder obtained an improvement for the same range of k by sharpening the analysis of the original\nPPSZ algorithm [Sch24, Theorem 1.5]. For general 3-SAT, a preprint by Jiang and Cai gives a\nrandomized O∗ (1.307031578n ) bound [JC26, Corollary 1.2], where O∗ suppresses factors polynomial\nin the input size. Liu’s deterministic algorithm combines branching with derandomized local search\nand solves 3-SAT in O(1.32793n ) time [Liu18, Theorem 21].\n   Randomized local search has also produced effective heuristics. The WalkSAT heuristic chooses an\nunsatisfied clause and flips one of its variables, combining random choices with greedy moves [SKC94].\nExperiments on formulas from circuit synthesis and diagnosis demonstrated the practical value of\nthis approach [SKC94, pp. 340–342, Tables 4–5].\n\f4                                                OPENAI\n\n\n   Among uniformly random k-CNFs, instances at the satisfiability threshold were conjectured\nto be the hardest for deciding satisfiability [VW21, Section 1]; for fixed k ⩾ 3 and lower clause\ndensities m/n ⩽ 2k /k C , where C is a universal constant, polynomial-time almost-uniform sampling\nof satisfying assignments is known with high probability over the formula [CLWYY25, Theorem 1.2].\nFor a uniformly random formula with polynomially many clauses, conditioned on satisfiability, Vyas\nand Williams [VW21, Theorem 3] give a PPZ-based algorithm that finds a solution in 2n(1−Ω(log k/k))\ntime with high probability over both the formula and the algorithm’s random choices.\n   The Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH) asserted that 3-SAT requires 2Ω(n) deterministic time\nin the worst case, and hence so does k-SAT for every fixed k ⩾ 3 [IP01, Theorem 1]. The Strong\nExponential Time Hypothesis (SETH) made the sharper prediction that k-SAT requires 2n(1−ok (1))\ntime as k grows. Precisely, for every ε ∈ (0, 1), there is a fixed k for which no deterministic algorithm\nsolves k-SAT in 2(1−ε)n (n + m)O(1) time. The running-time bounds for Schöning’s algorithm and\nPPSZ helped motivate this prediction: their improvements over exhaustive search diminish as k\ngrows [IP01, Open Problems, item 2].\n   The Gap Exponential Time Hypothesis (Gap-ETH) strengthens ETH by postulating constants\nδ, γ > 0 for which no O(2γn )-time algorithm can distinguish a satisfiable 3-CNF from one in which\nevery assignment violates at least a δ fraction of the clauses, even when m = O(n) [Din16, MR17].\nThe randomized version rules out an algorithm with the same running time that distinguishes\nthe two cases with probability at least 2/3 on every promised input [CCK+ 20, Conjecture 2.5].\nIt has been used to prove hardness of approximating Label Cover [Din16] and dense constraint\nsatisfaction problems [MR17]. It also rules out nontrivial fixed-parameter approximations for Clique\nand Dominating Set. For Clique, the excluded ratios are o(OPT), where OPT is the maximum\nclique size [CCK+ 20, Corollaries 5.3 and 5.6].\n   The Nondeterministic Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis (NSETH) asserts that, for every ε > 0,\nsome fixed k admits no nondeterministic 2(1−ε)n (n + m)O(1) -time algorithm for deciding whether\na k-CNF is unsatisfiable, or equivalently whether a k-DNF is a tautology [CGI+ 16]. NSETH has\nbeen used to establish barriers to deterministic fine-grained reductions from SAT to problems such\nas 3SUM and All-Pairs Shortest Paths [CGI+ 16], and to delineate SETH-based lower bounds for\napproximating directed graph diameter [Li21].\n   Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing whether the exact count\nis positive or zero decides both satisfiability and unsatisfiability in deterministic 2o(n) (n+m)Ok (1) time\nfor every fixed k; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise problem. The deterministic\nalgorithm also refutes the bounded-error randomized versions of ETH [DHM+ 14, Section 1] and\nSETH [CDL+ 16, Definition 2.1], and of Gap-ETH. It does not conflict with unconditional exponential\nlower bounds for restricted proof systems such as regular resolution [BI13]. In 2016, Ryan Williams\nrefuted the Merlin–Arthur version of SETH (MA-SETH) by giving a protocol that certifies k-CNF\nunsatisfiability in 2n/2 poly(n, m) time [Wil16, Theorem 3.3].\n\n\n1.1.2. Exact counting and counting complexity. In Valiant’s framework, #SAT and #3-SAT are\n#P-complete under parsimonious reductions [Val79, Section 3, Facts 1–2]. For fixed k ⩾ 3, previous\nexact-counting algorithms achieve polyk (n, m)2n−Ω(n/k) time, with a Las Vegas expected-time\nbound in [IMP12, Lemma 3.5] and a deterministic bound in [CW21, Theorem 1.6]. For #2-SAT,\nWahlström’s algorithm counts satisfying assignments in O∗ (1.2377n ) time [Wah08]. For #3-SAT,\nKutzkov uses complementary branching to count satisfying assignments in O∗ (1.637n ) time [Kut26].\nBoth algorithms are deterministic.\n   The counting Exponential Time Hypothesis (#ETH) asserted that exactly counting the satisfying\nassignments of a 3-CNF requires 2Ω(n) deterministic time [DHM+ 14, Section 1]. Under #ETH,\nCurticapean [Cur18] rules out deterministic 2o(v) -time algorithms for counting perfect matchings\neven in unweighted bipartite graphs with v vertices and O(v) edges. The perfect-matching algorithm\n\f                            THE EXPONENTIAL TIME HYPOTHESIS IS FALSE                                   5\n\n\nin Corollary 1.3 applies in particular to these graphs. Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1) solves #3-SAT\ndeterministically in 2o(n) poly(n, m) time and therefore refutes #ETH.\n1.1.3. Implications for fine-grained complexity. ETH and SETH became standard assumptions for\nparameterized and fine-grained complexity [IPZ01, VW19]. ETH-based reductions yielded lower\nbounds for the dependence on solution size in Clique [CHKX06], and on treewidth in constraint\nsatisfaction and partitioned subgraph isomorphism [Mar10]. SETH supported sharp exponential\nbounds for Hitting Set and Set Splitting [CDL+ 16], and for Independent Set and Dominating Set\nparameterized by treewidth [LMS18]. It also gave bounds on possible speedups of pseudopolynomial\nalgorithms for Subset Sum and Bicriteria Path [ABHS19].\n   For problems in P, SETH was used to rule out polynomial-factor speedups over quadratic running\ntimes for edit distance [BI18], longest common subsequence and dynamic time warping [BK15],\nFréchet distance [Bri14], and regular-expression matching [BI16]. Other applications concerned\napproximating graph diameter [RV13] and maintaining reachability counts or the size of the largest\nstrongly connected component under graph updates [AW14]. Together these results made SETH a\ncommon basis for explaining the apparent optimality of algorithms across quite different problems.\n   Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis for the hardness claims above. Some\nof the same lower bounds follow from weaker hypotheses that may still hold. For example, the\nlower bounds for longest common subsequence and regular-expression matching follow from the\nOrthogonal Vectors Conjecture (OVC) [BK15, BI16]. OVC asserts that for every fixed ε > 0, no\nO(N 2−ε poly(d))-time algorithm can decide whether two sets of N Boolean vectors of dimension\nd contain an orthogonal pair, one vector from each set. The reduction from SAT to Orthogonal\nVectors shows that SETH implies OVC [Wil05, VW19]. The lower bounds for Fréchet distance,\ngraph diameter approximation, and the treewidth dependence of Independent Set and Dominating\nSet also follow from the hypothesis that unrestricted CNF-SAT admits no 2(1−δ)n poly(n, m)-time\nalgorithm for any constant δ > 0 [Bri14, RV13, LMS18]. Theorem 1.1 gives a running-time guarantee\nfor each fixed clause width k, while this hypothesis allows the width to grow with n.\n   To obtain a faster algorithm for a target problem from our SAT algorithm, we need a reduction in\nthe other direction, from that problem to fixed-width SAT, that controls the number of variables.\nSet Splitting with sets of size at most k has such a reduction [CDL+ 16, Observation 3.6]: use one\nBoolean variable per element to specify its side of the split. For each set, one clause requires at least\none of its variables to be true, and a second requires at least one to be false. The resulting k-CNF\nhas one variable per universe element, so for fixed k Theorem 1.1 gives subexponential time in the\nuniverse size, up to a polynomial input-size factor.\n1.1.4. Prior work on algebraic algorithms for combinatorial problems. Our algorithm is algebraic,\nbased on polynomial interpolation of carefully chosen auxiliary variables. Below we discuss prior work\non using algebraic methods to design algorithm for combinatorial problems. For broader background,\nsee the surveys of Koutis and Williams [KW16] on algebraic fingerprints, Williams [Wil14] on the\npolynomial method in algorithm design, and Husfeldt [Hus11] on algorithmic inclusion–exclusion.\n   Williams’s split-and-list algorithm [Wil05] partitions the variables of a 2-CSP into three groups,\nenumerates partial assignments, and combines them using fast matrix multiplication. Koivisto [Koi06]\ngives a direct sum–product formulation. The algorithm of Sections 3–6 likewise enumerates as-\nsignments within groups. Fixing the shared measurements ⃗y makes their contributions multiply\nindependently. Scott and Sorkin [SS09, Section 6] extend split-and-list to polynomial-valued weights\nand arbitrary rings. Our summation routine similarly uses truncated polynomial rings to retain\nseveral coefficients in one computation.\n   Björklund [Bjö16, Sections 2–3] uses inclusion–exclusion and polynomial interpolation to reduce\npermanent and directed Hamiltonian-cycle counting to a weighted sum over smaller instances. He\nadds the weights of identical instances in a table, then solves each distinct instance with nonzero\ntotal weight once. Björklund, Kaski, and Williams [BKW19] use low-degree curves and generalized\n\f6                                              OPENAI\n\n\nKakeya sets to recover polynomial values from evaluations on a small structured set, yielding faster\nalgorithms for fermionants, including the permanent of integer matrices whose entries are bounded\nin absolute value by a fixed constant. Our polynomial replacements serve a similar purpose: they\nconnect the desired count to structured evaluations from which it can be reconstructed. Here the\nuseful structure is separation into groups after fixing a few shared measurements.\n   Bhargava, Ghosh, Kumar, and Mohapatra [BGKM23, Sections 3.1–3.2] evaluate multivariate\npolynomials over finite fields along low-degree curves, using Hasse derivatives and Hermite interpola-\ntion when values alone do not suffice. For a univariate restriction g, the order-j Hasse derivative at\nt is the coefficient of z j in g(t + z). We use the same recovery principle: compute several Taylor\ncoefficients at each point admitting fast summation, then recover the original count by Hermite\ninterpolation.\n   Woodruff and Yekhanin [WY07] use values and first derivatives along a line to recover a target\nvalue in private information retrieval. Kopparty, Saraf, and Yekhanin [KSY14] use higher-order\nHasse derivatives for local correction of multiplicity codes, extending the line-based recovery of\nReed–Muller codes. In our algorithm, the matrix construction supplies low-degree replacement forms\nthat let us compute the Taylor coefficients at the interpolation points by fast summation after fixing\na few shared measurements.\n1.1.5. Antecedents of the matrix construction. Our counting algorithm evaluates sums of products\nof polynomials over Boolean assignments. The polynomials share variables, so these sums do not\nfactor into independent smaller sums. We introduce linear substitutions, depending polynomially on\nan interpolation parameter, that make such a factorization possible after fixing a few integer linear\nmeasurements. An auxiliary square rational matrix M0 underlies these substitutions: a bound on\nrank(I − M0 ) limits the number of measurements, while restrictions on the nonzero entries of M0\nkeep the substitution degrees small. Section 2.7 explains this connection.\n   Related interpolation methods have been used to obtain low-rank approximations to structured\nmatrices. In [AW17, Section 3], a low-degree polynomial matches (−1)j on an interval of integer\ninner products j, using the interpolation lemma of [AW15, Lemma 3.1]. This gives low-rank\nmatrices agreeing with Walsh–Hadamard matrices in most entries, a result in the study of matrix\nrigidity [Val77]. Related interpolation gives rigidity bounds for Kronecker powers in [AGP23,\nTheorem 6.1]. Dvir and Liu [DL20, Claim 2.20 and Lemmas 3.3–3.4] use a spectral approach: sparse\nchanges to a function invariant under permutations of its coordinates make many Fourier coefficients\nvanish, reducing the rank of its group-circulant matrix.\n   Our construction also interpolates (−1)j on a short interval, now to obtain a polynomial in\ncommuting permutation matrices. The same polynomial gives both the rank bound for I − M0\nand the restrictions on the nonzero entries of M0 . We prove these properties and their quantitative\nbounds directly in Section 4.\n1.1.6. Sparsification and parity tests. We use the counting-preserving version [DHM+ 14, Appendix A]\nof the sparsification lemma [IPZ01], with quantitative bounds from [CIP06, Section 6] and [Cal09,\nLemma 5.14 and equation (5.4)]. This partitions the satisfying assignments among sparse formulas\non the same Boolean variables. Appendix A records the disjoint branching argument and the bounds\nneeded when its parameter varies with the input size.\n   The initial parity tests are the constraints of a Tanner code [Tan81, Section II], applied to the\nbinary vector recording which clauses an assignment violates. We use the edge-based expander-code\nconstruction of [SS96, Sections IV and VI]: each graph edge carries one bit, and each vertex requires\nits incident bits to belong to a fixed binary linear code. The local code’s minimum distance, together\nwith graph expansion, forces every nonzero vector satisfying all these constraints to have large\nsupport. This construction and distance argument are standard. Lemma 6.1 supplies the parameter\nchoices needed here, including the bounds on the number and support size of the tests, and constructs\nthe graph and local code by deterministic exhaustive searches. The graph is the bipartite double\n\f                               THE EXPONENTIAL TIME HYPOTHESIS IS FALSE                                          7\n\n\ncover of a Cayley multigraph. The shifts defining that multigraph nearly balance every nonconstant\nparity on the base group: this is the small-bias condition [NN93, AR94], stated explicitly in (6.2).\nFixing unused edge bits to zero gives a code on exactly m coordinates. Additional parity tests\nexclude the remaining nonzero violation vectors. We choose these tests by the averaging principle\nunderlying the method of conditional expectations [Rag88], using exact counts to evaluate each\ncandidate. Section 6 gives the constructions and quantitative bounds needed here.\n   Theorem 1.1 invites a renewed search for algorithms for problems whose known hardness results\nrelied on ETH or SETH. The examples in Section 1.1.3 suggest both immediate consequences and\nproblems for which new algorithmic ideas are needed.\n\n1.2. Notation. For an integer s ⩾ 0, write [s] = {1, . . . , s}, with [0] = ∅. For a condition E,\nthe scalar 1{E} equals one if E holds and zero otherwise. We use natural logarithms, writing\nlog2 when the base is two. For complexity bounds, poly(t1 , . . . , ts ) denotes a bound of the form\n(1+t1 +· · ·+ts )O(1) , with absolute implicit constants. Subscripts, as in polyk,c (t1 , . . . , ts ), allow those\nconstants to depend on the indicated fixed parameters. Integers are stored in signed binary notation,\nso an integer z uses O(log(|z| + 2)) bits. Rational numbers are stored as an integer numerator and a\npositive integer denominator; stated bit-length bounds apply to each of them. Space is measured in\nbits. Arithmetic-operation counts are distinguished from bit-operation counts.\n   For a finite set S, vectors in QS have coordinates indexed by S and are column vectors unless\nindicated otherwise. For U ⊆ S, the indicator vector 1U ∈ {0, 1}S has entries (1U )v = 1{v∈U } for\nv ∈ S. We write ⃗0 for the zero vector. For v ∈ S, the row δv ∈ Q1×S selects coordinate v, so\nδv x = xv . A vector indexed by U ⊆ S is extended to S by putting zero in each coordinate of S \\ U .\n   We write X ⃗ = (X1 , . . . , Xn ) for the vector of formal variables and use arrows for polynomial\ncoefficient vectors such as P⃗f,v (T ). For a coefficient row p⃗ = (pi )i∈S and a vector z = (zi )i∈S , we\n                                 p, z⟩ = i∈S pi zi . In particular, ⟨P⃗f,v (T ), ⃗\n                                         P\nwrite their scalar pairing as ⟨⃗                                                X⟩ is a scalar linear form in\nthe variables Xi .\n   For a polynomial P (X,  ⃗ z) in the variables X  ⃗ and a variable z, [z j ]P denotes the coefficient of\nz , viewed as a polynomial in ⃗\n j                                  X, and degX⃗ P denotes total degree in ⃗     X. The degree of a row of\npolynomials is the largest degree of any entry. Unless otherwise specified, polynomial coefficients are\nrational and matrix kernels are taken over Q.\n\n\n                                          2. Technique overview\n   We first count satisfying assignments of a 3-CNF in which each variable occurs in at most three\nclauses. The product of its clause polynomials has degree at most 3n, so we can use interpolation\nto recover the exact count from sums over small groups of variables. Section 2.8 then uses disjoint\nsparsification and variable copying to count the satisfying assignments of an arbitrary k-CNF in\n(n + m)Ok (1) 2o(n) time for every fixed k ⩾ 2. Taking k = 3 already refutes ETH. Section 2.9 explains\nhow counting on the original variables gives the exponent Ok (ρ(n)) of Proposition 6.4.\n\n2.1. The bounded-occurrence setting. Let F have n declared Boolean variables. Each clause\ncontains at most three literals, and each variable appears in at most three clauses, counting positive\nand negative occurrences together. Our task is to count the satisfying assignments of F exactly.\n   We remove tautological clauses and repeated literals within a clause. An empty clause gives count\nzero immediately. These operations preserve the occurrence bound. For each remaining clause e, let\nIe ⊆ [n] be its variable set. For each variable that occurs in no clause, add a factor equal to one\nwith scope {v}. Such a factor leaves the count unchanged and ensures that every variable has an\nassigned position in the construction. We call these constant-one terms dummy clauses. Let F index\n\f8                                                  OPENAI\n\n\nall clauses, including the dummy clauses. Then\n                                                                               X\n                  |Ie | ⩽ 3,     #{e ∈ F : v ∈ Ie } ⩽ 3,            n ⩽ M :=         |Ie | ⩽ 3n.   (2.1)\n                                                                               e∈F\n\nAll sums below still range over the original n Boolean variables.\n\n2.2. A direct polynomial for the count. Write Le,v (Xv ) = Xv for a positive literal and Le,v (Xv ) =\n1 − Xv for a negative literal. The satisfaction polynomial of clause e is\n                                                     Y\n                               Φe ((Xv )v∈Ie ) = 1 −   (1 − Le,v (Xv )).\n                                                        v∈Ie\n\nFor example, xa ∨ ¬xb ∨ xc gives\n\n                               Φe (Xa , Xb , Xc ) = 1 − (1 − Xa )Xb (1 − Xc ).\n\nOn Boolean inputs this polynomial is one when the clause is satisfied and zero otherwise. For a\ndummy clause e, put Φe = 1. Therefore\n                                        X Y\n                               #F =             Φe ((xv )v∈Ie ).                           (2.2)\n                                            x∈{0,1}n e∈F\n\nThe product has total degree at most M ⩽ 3n. This linear degree bound is the useful consequence\nof the three-occurrence assumption.\n   Equation (2.2) is an exact expression for the count, but evaluating its 2n summands would be\nexhaustive search. To motivate the algorithm, suppose we were given a partition of the variables\ninto r groups S1 , . . . , Sr satisfying both of the following conditions:\n    (1) Each group contains at most 2n/r variables.\n    (2) For every clause, all of its variables belong to a single group.\nThis is a hypothetical, very restrictive assumption; we do not assume that the input formula admits\nsuch a partition.\n   Under this assumption, we could count satisfying assignments in each group independently. Let\nNg be the number of assignments to the variables in Sg that satisfy all clauses in that group. Any\nsuch assignment can be combined with any satisfying assignments in the other groups, because no\nclause connects different groups. Every global satisfying assignment arises in exactly one such way.\nConsequently,\n                                                       r\n                                                       Y\n                                              #F =           Ng .\n                                                       g=1\n\nComputing each Ng by exhaustive search within its group examines a total of at most\n                                            r\n                                            X\n                                                  2|Sg | ⩽ r 22n/r\n                                            g=1\n\nlocal assignments, with polynomial work per assignment. For large r, this is much faster than\nenumerating all 2n global assignments.\n   We will replace the clause inputs so that different groups interact only through a small collection\nof shared linear summaries. Once the values of those summaries are fixed, we can again sum over\neach group separately.\n\f                             THE EXPONENTIAL TIME HYPOTHESIS IS FALSE   