er iteration of the three\n710\tenumerations above.\n711\t   For the bit-complexity and space bounds, see Appendix B.4.\n712\t\n713\t2.7. Constructing the low-degree replacement linear forms. Consider one occurrence of Xv\n714\tin a clause. At T = 0 its replacement must equal Xv . At each t = 1, . . . , Q, the clause must be able\n715\tto evaluate that same replacement using only its assigned group and a few shared measurements.\n716\tChoosing a different linear form at each point and interpolating would generally cost degree Q. To\n717\tkeep Taylor recovery cheap, we need degree R much smaller than Q.\n718\t   Suppose a term in the replacement uses a variable outside the clause’s assigned group only at\n719\tt = 2. Multiplying the term by 1 − T /2 leaves it unchanged at zero and removes it at t = 2. At every\n720\tother evaluation point the clause can read the variable from its own group. The same trick works for\n721\tseveral such points: one factor per point, at a cost of one degree each. The challenge is therefore to\n722\twrite each input as a sum in which the shared measurements determine one part, and each remaining\n723\tterm uses a variable outside the assigned group at only a few points. We first construct such a\n724\tdecomposition on auxiliary coordinates that record the group choices, then substitute the variables\n725\tof the formula.\n726\t\f14                                                OPENAI\n727\t\n728\t\n729\tStep 1: Set up the auxiliary coordinates.\n730\tAt each evaluation point, an occurrence (e, v) has two group choices: the group containing variable\n731\tv, and the group assigned to clause e. Across the Q points, these choices form a sequence of Q pairs.\n732\tWe therefore use the coordinate set\n733\t                                 B = ([r] × [r])Q ,        D = |B| = r2Q .\n734\tWrite an index as\n735\t                                                                            \u0001\n736\t                              u = (au (1), du 