                     x∈{0,1}n e∈F\n\nAt zero every replacement restores the original variable, while the degree of each clause polynomial\ngrows by at most a factor of R. Hence\n                                 H(0) = #F,         deg H ⩽ M R ⩽ 3nR.                                  (2.4)\nWe will recover H(0) from Taylor coefficients at t = 1, . . . , Q. The degree bound means that\nO(1 + nR/Q) coefficients per point suffice, as Section 2.5 explains. Our goal is therefore to make\nR small relative to Q: many points with low-degree replacements require fewer coefficients at each\npoint.\n   At a nonzero t, the value ⟨P⃗e,v (t), x⟩ of a replacement linear form need not lie in {0, 1}, even when\nx is Boolean. The transformed clause polynomials therefore need not act as satisfaction indicators.\n\n2.4. Separating the sum by fixing measurements. Fix t ∈ [Q]. We are given the coefficient\nvectors P⃗e,v (t), whose entries are now constants. We explain what structure lets us compute H(t)\nquickly, and how to use it. Throughout this subsection, write Sg = St,g and Cg = Ct,g .\n\nDefinition 2.1 ((r, τ, D)-design). For the given coefficient vectors P⃗e,v (t), an (r, τ, D)-design at t\nconsists of groups Sg , clause assignments σe (t), and measurement matrices Cg satisfying the following\nfour conditions:\n    (1) Small groups. Partition the variables into disjoint groups [n] = S1 ⊔ · · · ⊔ Sr , with |Sg | ⩽ 2n/r.\n    (2) One assigned group per clause. Assign each clause e to one group σe (t) ∈ [r]. Condition 4\n        must hold with this same group for every replacement linear form of clause e.\n    (3) Few bounded measurements. Each group g has an integer matrix Cg with columns indexed\n        by Sg and cg rows. Each row specifies one linear measurement of the variables in Sg . There\n        are at most 2τ n rows in total, and the sum of absolute values of the coefficients in each row\n        is at most D. For an assignment x, write\n                            xg = x|Sg ,      yg = Cg xg ,        ⃗y = (y1 , . . . , yr ).\n    (4) Dependence on other groups only through measurements. Suppose clause e is assigned to\n        group g = σe (t). Each replacement linear form of this clause may depend freely on the\n        variables in Sg . Its dependence on any other group Sh must be a linear combination of\n\f10                                                OPENAI\n\n\n        that group’s measurements yh . Precisely, for every v ∈ Ie , there must be coefficient vectors\n       ⃗λe,v,h ∈ Qch , for h ̸= g, such that, for every x ∈ Qn ,\n                                                                      X\n                              ⟨P⃗e,v (t), x⟩ = ⟨P⃗e,v (t)|Sg , xg ⟩ +   ⟨⃗λe,v,h , yh ⟩.         (2.5)\n                                                                  h̸=g\n\n       Equivalently, P⃗e,v (t)|Sh lies in the row space of Ch for every h ̸= g.\n  The matrices Ch are shared by all clauses. A clause’s assigned group need not contain its original\nvariables. Once ⃗y is fixed, however, the entire transformed clause polynomial depends only on the\nvariables in its assigned group.\nThe algorithm. Given an (r, τ, D)-design at t, first find the coefficient vectors ⃗λe,v,h by solving\nthe linear systems guaranteed by condition 4. For fixed ⃗y , let Xt,g (yg ) be the assignments to the\nvariables in Sg whose measurements equal yg :\n                                Xt,g (yg ) = {u ∈ {0, 1}Sg : Cg u = yg }.\nFor such an assignment u, compute the contribution of group g as follows. For each clause e assigned\nto g, first use (2.5) to compute the values of its replacement linear forms from u and the fixed\nmeasurements. Next evaluate the clause polynomial Φe on these values. Finally, multiply the values\nobtained from all clauses assigned to g. Denote this product by At,g (u; ⃗y ). No assignments to the\nother groups are needed for this calculation.\n  Each measurement of a Boolean assignment is an integer in [−D, D]. Initialize Z 