  D = r ⩽ (2n)           .\n                                                     r log n\nWe first show how these bounds give a fast counting algorithm; the next subsection will justify that\nthey can all be achieved simultaneously.\n   Recall that q = J − 1 = ⌊3nR/Q⌋. For each evaluation\n                                                    \u0001 point t, Section 2.5 computes one restricted\nsum for each pair U ⊆ E with |E| ⩽ q. There are nj choices of an E of size j, and then 2j choices\nof U . Each restricted sum enumerates measurement tuples and assignments within individual groups.\n\f                           THE EXPONENTIAL TIME HYPOTHESIS IS FALSE                                 13\n\n\nThus the three costs multiply to give, up to polynomial factors,\n                              min(q,n) \u0012 \u0013\n                                X       n\n                                     2   j\n                                           · (2D + 1)2τ n · |22n/r\n                                                               {z } .                           (2.11)\n                                        j    |   {z     }\n                                j=0                        one group\n                              |     {z    } measurements\n                                Taylor data\n\nTo\u0001 bound the first cost, note that 1 ⩽ q ⩽ n/2 for sufficiently large n, since R/Q → 0. Using\n n          j                          j\n j ⩽ (en/j) and the fact that (2en/j) increases for 1 ⩽ j ⩽ n, we obtain\n                                    q\n                                                            2en q\n                                            \u0012 \u0013           \u0012    \u0013\n                                    X        n\n                                             j\n                                          2     ⩽ (q + 1)         .\n                                             j               q\n                                    j=0\n\nSince q = Θ(nR/Q), the logarithm of this cost is O(n(R/Q) log(2Q/R)). Taking logarithms of all\nthree costs in (2.11) therefore gives\n                                \u0012 \u0014                                \u0015\u0013\n                                      R     2Q                   1\n                              O n       log    + τ log(2D + 1) +      .\n                                      Q      R                   r\nEach term is small under (2.10):\n                                             \u0012                       \u0013\n                                R     2Q       log log n\n                                  log     =O √            · log log n ,\n                                Q      R          log n\n                                             \u0012                 \u0013\n                                                  1\n                           τ log(2D + 1) = O           · log n = O(1/r),\n                                               r log n\n                                               (log log n)2\n                                             \u0012               \u0013\n                                        1\n                                          =O      √             .\n                                        r           log n\nThus the bounded-occurrence instance can be counted in\n                                                             2\n                                                                 √\n                                         nO(1) 2O(n(log log n) / log n)                         (2.12)\nbit operations and polynomial space. The finitely many smaller inputs are handled by exhaustive\nsearch.\n   The number of points Q, the number of groups r, and the truncation order J = O(1 + nR/Q) are\npolynomial in n. Hence the loops over points and groups, arithmetic on polynomials of degree less\nthan J, and the final interpolation add only polynomially many operations per iteration of the three\nenumerations above.\n   For the bit-complexity and space bounds, see Appendix B.4.\n\n2.7. Constructing the low-degree replacement linear forms. Consider one occurrence of Xv\nin a clause. At T = 0 its replacement must equal Xv . At each t = 1, . . . , Q, the clause must be able\nto evaluate that same replacement using only its assigned group and a few shared measurements.\nChoosing a different linear form at each point and interpolating would generally cost degree Q. To\nkeep Taylor recovery cheap, we need degree R much smaller than Q.\n   Suppose a term in the replacement uses a variable outside the clause’s assigned group only at\nt = 2. Multiplying the term by 1 − T /2 leaves it unchanged at zero and removes it at t = 2. At every\nother evaluation point the clause can read the variable from its own group. The same trick works for\nseveral such points: one factor per point, at a cost of one degree each. The challenge is therefore to\nwrite each input as a sum in which the shared measurements determine one part, and each remaining\nterm uses a variable outside the assigned group at only a few points. We first construct such a\ndecomposition on auxiliary coordinates that record the group choices, then substitute the variables\nof the formula.\n\f14                                                OPENAI\n\n\nStep 1: Set up the auxiliary coordinates.\nAt each evaluation point, an occurrence (e, v) has two group choices: the group containing variable\nv, and the group assigned to clause e. Across the Q points, these choices form a sequence of Q pairs.\nWe therefore use the coordinate set\n                                 B = ([r] × [r])Q ,        D = |B| = r2Q .\nWrite an index as\n                                                                            \u0001\n                              u = (au (1), du (1)), . . . , (au (Q), du (Q)) .\nWe will construct one replacement row for every such index, so it will be available whatever group\nchoices we later make for the formula.\n   Let ζ⃗ = (ζw )w∈B be formal placeholders for the original variables that we will insert in Step 4. At\npoint t, coordinate ζw belongs to group aw (t), whereas the replacement for ζu is assigned to group\ndu (t). Thus the coordinates that this replacement may use freely are\n                               St,du (t) ,   St,g := {w ∈ B : aw (t) = g}.\nIn particular, ζw belongs to the assigned group exactly when aw (t) = du (t). The sets St,g partition\nB, with D/r coordinates in each group.\n   Our auxiliary goal is to construct coefficient rows P⃗u (T ) ∈ Q[T ]D and one matrix\n                                                                      Dτ\n                                     C ∈ {0, 1, −1}c×D ,         c⩽      ,\n                                                                      3r\nsuch that\n                                 ⟨P⃗u (0), ⃗\n                                           ζ⟩ = ζu ,    deg P⃗u ⩽ R − 1,\nand, at every t ∈ [Q], the replacement linear form ⟨P⃗u (t), ⃗\n                                                             ζ⟩ is a linear combination of the available\ndata\n                                         ⃗S\n                                        ζ|           and C ζ.⃗                                    (2.13)\n                                            t,du (t)\n\n\nThe same measurements C ζ⃗ serve every row and every evaluation point. This is the auxiliary\ncounterpart of the dependence condition in the design from Section 2.4.\nStep 2: Construct the constant matrix.\nWe start from the identity\n                                                           X\n                                ζu = ((ID − M0 )⃗\n                                                ζ)u +            M0 [u, w]ζw .\n                                                           w∈B\n\nWe want the first term to be determined by C ζ.   ⃗ In the sum, each nonzero coefficient should\nconnect incompatible groups at only a few points, so that a low-degree pol