cation [IPZ01],\n995\twith quantitative bounds from [CIP06, Section 6] and [Cal09, Lemma 5.14 and equation (5.4)].\n996\tAs recorded in Lemma 6.3, this expresses the input count as the sum of the counts of at most\n997\tpolyk (n, σ −1 )2σn formulas G. Each G has the same n declared variables, with at most\n998\t                                            \u0010                   \u0011\n999\t                                    B = Ok (σ −1 log(2/σ))k−1\n1000\toccurrences per variable, and hence at most Bn nonempty clauses. An empty clause gives count\n1001\tzero immediately.\n1002\t   First turn G into a 3-CNF without changing its count. Keep clauses of size at most three. For\n1003\teach longer clause, compute its disjunction with binary OR gates and require the final output to be\n1004\tone. For a gate z = u ∨ v, impose\n1005\t                                 (¬u ∨ z) ∧ (¬v ∨ z) ∧ (u ∨ v ∨ ¬z).\n1006\tHere u, v are input literals or earlier gate outputs, and z is fresh. These clauses enforce equality\n1007\twith the OR, so the gate values are uniquely determined by the original assignment. Consequently,\n1008\ta satisfying assignment of G has exactly one extension to the new formula, and an assignment not\n1009\tsatisfying G has none. This uses O(k) gates per clause.\n1010\t   Next reduce the occurrences. If a variable x occurs s ⩾ 2 times in this 3-CNF, replace its occur-\n1011\trences by distinct copies x1 , . . . , xs , preserving their signs, and add Tovey’s implication cycle [Tov84,\n1012\tp. 86]:\n1013\t                           (¬x1 ∨ x2 ) ∧ · · · ∧ (¬xs−1 ∨ xs ) ∧ (¬xs ∨ x1 ).\n1014\tThe cycle forces all copies to agree. Each copy appears in one clause inherited from the 3-CNF\n1015\tand two cycle clauses, hence in exactly three clauses. Variables with at most one occurrence are\n1016\tkept unchanged, including unused declared variables. This standard variable-copy reduction [Gol08,\n1017\t\f                            THE EXPONENTIAL TIME HYPOTHESIS IS FALSE                               19\n1018\t\n1019\t\n1020\tProposition 4.7] therefore also gives a bijection between satisfying assignments. The final formula G\n1021\t                                                                                                    b\n1022\thas #Gb = #G and\n1023\t                               n ⩽ N := #{variables of G} b = Ok (Bn).\n1024\tAll its clauses have size at most three, including the two-literal cycle clauses, and every variable\n1025\toccurs at most three times. Thus it is exactly an instance of the setting of this overview.\n1026\t  Apply (2.12) to each G b and add the counts. When σ −1 is bounded by a fixed power of log n, so is\n1027\tB, and the resulting time is\n1028\t                                                                       2\n1029\t                                                                           √\n1030\t                               (n + m)Ok (1) 2Ok(σn+Bn(log log n) / log n) .\n1031\tFor example, choosing σ within a constant factor of (log n)−1/(2k) yields\n1032\t                                                k+1 /(log n)1/(2k)\n1033\t                   (n + m)Ok (1) 2Ok(n(log log n)                    ) = (n + m)Ok (1) 2o(n)\n1034\t\n1035\twith polynomial space, processing the sparsification tree and the reduced instances one at a time.\n1036\tThe parameter σ may be chosen rational.\n1037\t\n1038\t2.9. A faster algorithm for #k-SAT via counting solutions to sparse low-degree poly-\n1039\tnomial equations over  √ F2 . The running-time exponent in Section 2.8 is Ok (σn + Bρ(n)), where\n1040\tρ(n) = n(log log n)2 / log n. If sparsification gave B = Ok (1) at σ = Θ(ρ(n)/n), we would already\n1041\thave a 2Ok (ρ(n)) -time algorithm for general #k-SAT up to polynomial factors: copying would use\n1042\tonly Ok (n) variables per leaf, and the number of leaves would fit within the same bound. At this\n1043\tchoice of σ, however, the guarantee is only B = (log n)Ok (1) .\n1044\t   We obtain the 2Ok (ρ(n)) bound in two steps. First, the counting algorithm of this overview extends\n1045\tto systems of O(n) polynomial equations over F2 , each of degree at most k with polylogarithmically\n1046\tmany monomials (Theorem 2.2). Second, even if a leaf G has Bn clauses, we can construct such a\n1047\tsystem on the original n variables whose common zeros are exactly the satisfying assignments of G.\n1048\tWe illustrate the reduction first with a self-contained randomized construction, then explain the\n1049\tcounting algorithm.\n1050\tA randomized reduction to sparse polynomial equations. Write ve (x) = 1 if xPviolates clause\n1051\te, and 0 otherwise. A parity test on a set S of clauses is the polynomial equation e∈S ve (x) = 0\n1052\tover F2 : it accepts if x violates an even number of clauses in S. Every satisfying assignment passes,\n1053\tso we need only catch every nonsatisfying one. If G has mG ⩽ n clauses, one test per clause suffices.\n1054\tSuppose mG > n.\n1055\t   A short test can easily miss a single violated clause, but all assignments that violate only that\n1056\tclause give identical answers. Thus there are only mG singleton patterns to catch. Use 8n independent\n1057\ttests. For each, draw L = 2B⌈log2 (2mG )⌉ clauses uniformly with replacement. For each draw,\n1058\tindependently add its violation bit to the parity sum with probability 1/2; two included copies\n1059\tcancel. Since mG ⩽ Bn, each clause is drawn at least logarithmically often in expectation overall.\n1060\tFor a fixed set of w ⩾ 1 violated clauses, let u = Lw/mG be the expected number of hits per test. If\n1061\tthe sample hits the set, the coin on the first hit makes the parity odd with probability 1/2, even\n1062\twith all other coins fixed. Thus the rejection probability is\n1063\t                                pw = 12 1 − (1 − w/mG )L ⩾ 12 (1 − e−u ).\n1064\t                                                         \u0001\n1065\t\n1066\t  We count sets for few violations and assignments for many:\n1067\t    • If u ⩽ 1, then pw ⩾ u/4. There are at most mw   G sets of size w, so the chance that at least\n1068\t                                         w\n1069\t      one escapes all tests is at most mG e −8np   ⩽ mw  −2nLw/mG ⩽ 16−w . Summing over w ⩾ 1\n1070\t                                                      Ge\n1071\t                                                 w\n1072\t\n1073\t      gives failure probability at most 1/15.\n1074\t    • If u > 1, then pw > 1/4. A union bound over the 2n assignments bounds the chance that\n1075\t      any assignment in this case escapes by 2n (3/4)8n < 1/4.\n1076\t\f20                                                 OPENAI\n1077\t\n1078\t\n1079\tThus with probability greater than 2/3, the common zeros are exactly the satisfying assignments\n1080\tof G. Each ve is a product of at most k factors of the form xj or 1 + xj , so each test has degree\n1081\tat most k and at most 2k L monomials, polylogarithmic in n. Section 6 constructs short tests with\n1082\tthe same solution set deterministically. We first use parity checks from an expander code to ensure\n1083\tthat any nonsatisfying assignment still passing must violate many clauses. A random short test\n1084\tthen catches each such assignment with probability at least 1/4. For each candidate test, we count\n1085\tthe assignments that pass both that test and all tests chosen so far. We append a candidate that\n1086\tminimizes this count: every satisfying assignment passes every candidate, so this eliminates at least\n1087\ta quarter of the remaining nonsatisfying assignments.\n1088\tCounting the common zeros. Recall from Section 2.2 that Φe (x) is 1 if x satisfies clause e and 0\n1089\totherwise. What should replace this factor when we test a whole set Si of clauses at once? First\n1090\tcount how many clauses in this test are violated, using ordinary integer addition:\n1091\t                                       X            X              \u0001\n109