es only hD ⩽ 6n coordinate positions. We have therefore constructed, in\npolynomial time, replacement forms that restore every original input at zero, have degree at most\nR − 1 ⩽ R, and admit the promised (r, τ, D)-designs at every t ∈ [Q]. This supplies the bounds used\nin Section 2.6; Lemma 4.8 records the parameter check for the more general setting of the full proof.\n\n2.8. A weaker algorithm for general #k-SAT. The algorithm above already gives a subexpo-\nnential algorithm for general #k-SAT: after disjoint sparsification, we reduce each instance directly\nto a 3-CNF in which every variable occurs at most three times. Both steps preserve the count, as\nfollows.\n   Fix k ⩾ 2. We use the counting-preserving version [DHM+ 14, Appendix A] of sparsification [IPZ01],\nwith quantitative bounds from [CIP06, Section 6] and [Cal09, Lemma 5.14 and equation (5.4)].\nAs recorded in Lemma 6.3, this expresses the input count as the sum of the counts of at most\npolyk (n, σ −1 )2σn formulas G. Each G has the same n declared variables, with at most\n                                            \u0010                   \u0011\n                                    B = Ok (σ −1 log(2/σ))k−1\noccurrences per variable, and hence at most Bn nonempty clauses. An empty clause gives count\nzero immediately.\n   First turn G into a 3-CNF without changing its count. Keep clauses of size at most three. For\neach longer clause, compute its disjunction with binary OR gates and require the final output to be\none. For a gate z = u ∨ v, impose\n                                 (¬u ∨ z) ∧ (¬v ∨ z) ∧ (u ∨ v ∨ ¬z).\nHere u, v are input literals or earlier gate outputs, and z is fresh. These clauses enforce equality\nwith the OR, so the gate values are uniquely determined by the original assignment. Consequently,\na satisfying assignment of G has exactly one extension to the new formula, and an assignment not\nsatisfying G has none. This uses O(k) gates per clause.\n   Next reduce the occurrences. If a variable x occurs s ⩾ 2 times in this 3-CNF, replace its occur-\nrences by distinct copies x1 , . . . , xs , preserving their signs, and add Tovey’s implication cycle [Tov84,\np. 86]:\n                           (¬x1 ∨ x2 ) ∧ · · · ∧ (¬xs−1 ∨ xs ) ∧ (¬xs ∨ x1 ).\nThe cycle forces all copies to agree. Each copy appears in one clause inherited from the 3-CNF\nand two cycle clauses, hence in exactly three clauses. Variables with at most one occurrence are\nkept unchanged, including unused declared variables. This standard variable-copy reduction [Gol08,\n\f                            THE EXPONENTIAL TIME HYPOTHESIS IS FALSE                               19\n\n\nProposition 4.7] therefore also gives a bijection between satisfying assignments. The final formula G\n                                                                                                    b\nhas #Gb = #G and\n                               n ⩽ N := #{variables of G} b = Ok (Bn).\nAll its clauses have size at most three, including the two-literal cycle clauses, and every variable\noccurs at most three times. Thus it is exactly an instance of the setting of this overview.\n  Apply (2.12) to each G b and add the counts. When σ −1 is bounded by a fixed power of log n, so is\nB, and the resulting time is\n                                                                       2\n                                                                           √\n                               (n + m)Ok (1) 2Ok(σn+Bn(log log n) / log n) .\nFor example, choosing σ within a constant factor of (log n)−1/(2k) yields\n                                                k+1 /(log n)1/(2k)\n                   (n + m)Ok (1) 2Ok(n(log log n)                    ) = (n + m)Ok (1) 2o(n)\n\nwith polynomial space, processing the sparsification tree and the reduced instances one at a time.\nThe parameter σ may be chosen rational.\n\n2.9. A faster algorithm for #k-SAT via counting solutions to sparse low-degree poly-\nnomial equations over  √ F2 . The running-time exponent in Section 2.8 is Ok (σn + Bρ(n)), where\nρ(n) = n(log log n)2 / log n. If sparsification gave B = Ok (1) at σ = Θ(ρ(n)/n), we would already\nhave a 2Ok (ρ(n)) -time algorithm for general #k-SAT up to polynomial factors: copying would use\nonly Ok (n) variables per leaf, and the number of leaves would fit within the same bound. At this\nchoice of σ, however, the guarantee is only B = (log n)Ok (1) .\n   We obtain the 2Ok (ρ(n)) bound in two steps. First, the counting algorithm of this overview extends\nto systems of O(n) polynomial equations over F2 , each of degree at most k with polylogarithmically\nmany monomials (Theorem 2.2). Second, even if a leaf G has Bn clauses, we can construct such a\nsystem on the original n variables whose common zeros are exactly the satisfying assignments of G.\nWe illustrate the reduction first with a self-contained randomized construction, then explain the\ncounting algorithm.\nA randomized reduction to sparse polynomial equations. Write ve (x) = 1 if xPviolates clause\ne, and 0 otherwise. A parity test on a set S of clauses is the polynomial equation e∈S ve (x) = 0\nover F2 : it accepts if x violates an even number of clauses in S. Every satisfying assignment passes,\nso we need only catch every nonsatisfying one. If G has mG ⩽ n clauses, one test per clause suffices.\nSuppose mG > n.\n   A short test can easily miss a single violated clause, but all assignments that violate only that\nclause give identical answers. Thus there are only mG singleton patterns to catch. Use 8n independent\ntests. For each, draw L = 2B⌈log2 (2mG )⌉ clauses uniformly with replacement. For each draw,\nindependently add its violation bit to the parity sum with probability 1/2; two included copies\ncancel. Since mG ⩽ Bn, each clause is drawn at least logarithmically often in expectation overall.\nFor a fixed set of w ⩾ 1 violated clauses, let u = Lw/mG be the expected number of hits per test. If\nthe sample hits the set, the coin on the first hit makes the parity odd with probability 1/2, even\nwith all other coins fixed. Thus the rejection probability is\n                                pw = 12 1 − (1 − w/mG )L ⩾ 12 (1 − e−u ).\n                                                         \u0001\n\n  We count sets for few violations and assignments for many:\n    • If u ⩽ 1, then pw ⩾ u/4. There are at most mw   G sets of size w, so the chance that at least\n                                         w\n      one escapes all tests is at most mG e −8np   ⩽ mw  −2nLw/mG ⩽ 16−w . Summing over w ⩾ 1\n                                                      Ge\n                                                 w\n\n      gives failure probability at most 1/15.\n    • If u > 1, then pw > 1/4. A union bound over the 2n assignments bounds the chance that\n      any assignment in this case escapes by 2n (3/4)8n < 1/4.\n\f20                                                 OPENAI\n\n\nThus with probability greater than 2/3, the common zeros are exactly the satisfying assignments\nof G. Each ve is a product of at most k factors of the form xj or 1 + xj , so each test has degree\nat most k and at most 2k L monomials, polylogarithmic in n. Section 6 constructs short tests with\nthe same solution set deterministically. We first use parity checks from an expander code to ensure\nthat any nonsatisfying assignment still passing must violate many clauses. A random short test\nthen catches each such assignment with probability at least 1/4. For each candidate test, we count\nthe assignments that pass both that test and all tests chosen so far. We append a candidate that\nminimizes this count: every satisfying assignment passes every candidate, so this eliminates at least\na quarter of the remaining nonsatisfying assignments.\nCounting the common zeros. Recall from Section 2.2 that Φe (x) is 1 if x satisfies clause e and 0\notherwise. What should replace this factor when we test a whole set Si o