n2252\tO(n) sparse polynomial equations on its original n variables whose common zeros are exactly its\n2253\tsatisfying assignments. One equation per clause could require Bn equations. Instead, each equation\n2254\twill test the parity of the violations of a short list of clauses. We use Theorem 2.2 both to choose\n2255\tthese equations and to obtain the final count.\n2256\t   Let m be the number of indexed clauses and ve (x) the violation bit of clause e. As a polynomial\n2257\tover F2 , ve is a product of the factors 1 + xv for positive literals and xv for negative literals. A\n2258\ttautological clause has ve = 0 and an empty clause has ve = 1. After removing repeated literals,\n2259\tdeg ve ⩽ k and ve expands to at most 2k monomials. Put v(x) = (v1 (x), . . . , vm (x)). For a binary\n2260\tmatrix A with m columns, row i specifies the equation"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9be64548-7b53-4f99-81aa-4ed97f3b0de3","uuid":"c16c347c-8858-4e6e-bd23-e8202b8b9be9","timestamp":"2026-11-04T18:08:27.056Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/01_audit/eth.txt","content":"4.7. Choosing the parameters. In the application, both the largest scope w and the maximum\nnumber b of factors containing a variable will be bounded by a fixed power of log n. We now choose\nparameters satisfying Lemma 4.7 in that regime. The exponent of the counting cost depends on the\nthree quantities\n                               R      2Q                          1\n                                  log    ,      τ log(2D + 1),      .\n                               Q       R                          r\nThey account respectively for recovering Taylor coefficients, enumerating measurement values, and\nenumerating assignments in one group. The following choices bound all three at once.\nLemma 4.8. Fix an integer β ⩾ 1. Let n ⩾ 20, put λ = ⌈log2 n⌉ and ν = ⌈log2 λ⌉, and suppose\nb, w ⩽ λβ and n ⩽ M ⩽ bn. For all sufficientl