hese sums by\n                         Bsum = poly(n, mtest , ℓ, k, Q, log(D + 2), log(S + 2)).                      (5.6)\nFor each 0 ⩽ s < ℓ we interpolate the rational polynomial [V s ]H(T ) from its QJ Taylor coefficients.\nThe appendix verifies polynomial bit cost for this interpolation and the final integer remainder in\n(5.5). The O(QJℓ) stored coefficients, the design and the sequential enumerations in Proposition 3.4\ngive the stated space bound.                                                                       □\n5.5. Constructing the forms and completing the proof. Each sparse polynomial reads few\nvariables, but one variable might occur in many equations. We first fix the values of the few variables\nwith the largest numbers of occurrences. On each resulting branch we can construct the design\nrequired by Proposition 5.3.\nProof of Theorem 2.2. Let P be the supplied polynomial system. An empty list of equations gives\n2n common zeros; a constant equation 1 = 0 gives none. Discard equations 0 = 0. Include a neutral\nfactor equal to one with scope {v} for each declared variable v, and write\n                                                 X\n                                           M=       |If |\n                                                      f ∈F\n\nfor the total number of factor–variable incidences. The input bounds imply M = n(log n)Ok,c (1) , and\nevery scope has (log n)Ok,c (1) variables. A particular variable, however, may occur in many of these\nscopes.\n   Put λ = ⌈log2 n⌉ and\n                                                      M √\n                                                    \u0018 \u0019\n                                               b0 =       ⌈ λ⌉.\n                                                      n\nLet S ⊆ [n] be the variables belonging to more than b0 scopes. For each b ∈ {0, 1}S , we fix\nx|S = b and count the completions on [n]√\\ S; we call this restricted problem a branch. Since\n                    √\n|S|