{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017fRteBZQrhQ3Ct5yKthUgw","type":"tool_result","content":"2250\t                      6. Reducing #k-SAT to counting common zeros\n2251\t   Given a sparse k-CNF F with at most Bn clauses, where B is polylogarithmic in n, we construct\n2252\tO(n) sparse polynomial equations on its original n variables whose common zeros are exactly its\n2253\tsatisfying assignments. One equation per clause could require Bn equations. Instead, each equation\n2254\twill test the parity of the violations of a short list of clauses. We use Theorem 2.2 both to choose\n2255\tthese equations and to obtain the final count.\n2256\t   Let m be the number of indexed clauses and ve (x) the violation bit of clause e. As a polynomial\n2257\tover F2 , ve is a product of the factors 1 + xv for positive literals and xv for negative literals. A\n2258\ttautological clause has ve = 0 and an empty clause has ve = 1. After removing repeated literals,\n2259\tdeg ve ⩽ k and ve expands to at most 2k monomials. Put v(x) = (v1 (x), . . . , vm (x)). For a binary\n2260\tmatrix A with m columns, row i specifies the equation\n2261\t                                            X\n2262\t                                 Pi (x) :=       ve (x) = 0      over F2 .                       (6.1)\n2263\t                                          e:Aie =1\n2264\t\f40                                                OPENAI\n2265\t\n2266\t\n2267\tWrite PA for this system and N (A) = N (PA ) for its number of common zeros. Every satisfying\n2268\tassignment passes every row. Hence N (A) = #F + D(A), where D(A) counts the nonsatisfying\n2269\tassignments still passing. We do not know #F , but it is the same for every candidate next row: the\n2270\trow minimizing N also minimizes D.\n2271\t   We represent a test row by a list of pairs (e, c) with e ∈ [m] and c ∈ {0, 1}, summing coefficients\n2272\tmodulo 2 if a clause index is repeated. The length of this list counts all pairs, including repeated\n2273\tindices and pairs with coefficient zero. A row of list length s thus reads at most ks variables before\n2274\tcancellation.\n2275\t6.1. Initial tests that exclude small violation sets. If an assignment violates only one of many\n2276\tclauses, a test sampling a short list of clauses is unlikely to notice it. We therefore first construct\n2277\ta matrix A(0) that rejects every nonzero violation vector with small support. Every nonsatisfying\n2278\tassignment that passes these initial tests must violate many clauses. A further test that samples a\n2279\tshort list of clauses can then reject each such assignment with constant probability.\n2280\t   We use the standard edge-based Tanner code: bits are assigned to the edges of an expander, and\n2281\tthe bits incident to each vertex must form a word of a prescribed local linear code (see Tanner [Tan81,\n2282\tSection II] for the local constraints and Sipser and Spielman [SS96, Sections IV and VI] for the\n2283\texpander construction). The usual local-distance and expansion argument gives the needed lower\n2284\tbound on the weight of a nonzero codeword. The proof below constructs the graph and local code\n2285\tdeterministically, with parameters that meet our bounds on the number and length of the tests.\n2286\tLemma 6.1. Let n, m, B be positive integers with m ⩽ Bn, and put\n2287\t                   µ = ⌊log2 m⌋ + 1,        P = 20000B 2 µ2 ,      D0 = 320000B 2 µ3 .\n2288\tThere is a deterministic construction of a binary matrix A(0) with m columns and at most 3n rows\n2289\tsuch that every row has at most D0 nonzero entries and\n2290\t                 z ∈ {0, 1}m \\ {⃗0},    A(0) z = ⃗0   (mod 2)   =⇒      | supp z| ⩾ m/P.\n2291\tThe construction uses exp(poly(B, log(m + 2))) bit operations and poly(n, m, B) bits of working\n2292\tspace.\n2293\tProof. We construct a bipartite graph with between m and 2m edges, using its edges as temporary\n2294\tbinary coordinates. Checks at each vertex will force every vertex touched by a nonzero edge to have\n2295\tmany nonzero incident edges; a mixing bound will then force large total weight. At the end, we keep\n2296\tm edge coordinates and fix the rest to zero. Let\n2297\t                     dG = min(m, D0 ),         qG = min{2a : a ∈ Z⩾0 , 2a dG ⩾ m}.\n2298\tThen m ⩽ qG dG < 2m. Write qG = 2ℓG and take two copies of {0, 1}ℓG as the vertex sets. For each\n2299\tleft vertex u and each port j ∈ [dG ], put an edge from u to u ⊕ sj , where ⊕ denotes bitwise addition\n2300\tmodulo 2 and the shifts s1 , . . . , sdG ∈ {0, 1}ℓG will be chosen below. Edges are indexed by the pairs\n2301\t(u, j); parallel edges remain distinct. Every vertex on either side has one incident edge at each port\n2302\tj, so the graph is dG -regular.\n2303\t   To obtain the mixing estimate (6.3), we choose the shifts so that every parity of a nonempty set\n2304\tof coordinates is nearly balanced. For a nonzero vector b ∈ {0, 1}ℓG , the bit b · sj mod 2 is the parity\n2305\tof those coordinates of sj where b equals 1. With ε = 1/(200Bµ), we require\n2306\t                              dG\n2307\t                              X\n2308\t                                    (−1)b·sj ⩽ εdG       b ∈ {0, 1}ℓG \\ {⃗0} .\n2309\t                                                                            \u0001\n2310\t                                                                                                    (6.2)\n2311\t                              j=1\n2312\t\n2313\tThe sum in (6.2) is the number of indices j with even parity minus the number with odd parity.\n2314\tThus, for every nonzero b, each parity occurs at a fraction of indices between (1 − ε)/2 and (1 + ε)/2.\n2315\tA list of shifts with this property is called ε-biased [NN93, Definitions 2.1–2.2].\n2316\t\f                            THE EXPONENTIAL TIME HYPOTHESIS IS FALSE                                 41\n2317\t\n2318\t\n2319\t   If qG = 1, there is no condition to chec