es, and each normalized formula has a\n2514\tcount one leaf at a time. The current\n2515\tpolyk (n)-bit encoding. Since s ⩽ ⌈log2 n⌉, storing this path and counting one leaf use polyk (n, m)\n2516\tbits. Disjointness keeps the sum of the processed leaf counts between zero and 2n , so its accumulator\n2517\tuses at most n + 1 bits. Thus the total space is polyk (n, m) bits.                                 □\n2518\t\n2519\t                  Appendix A. Partitioning the satisfying assignments"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9be64548-7b53-4f99-81aa-4ed97f3b0de3","uuid":"8c829794-3853-4a10-8627-b38ab8e0ff27","timestamp":"2026-11-04T18:08:28.160Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/01_audit/eth.txt","content":"                      6. Reducing #k-SAT to counting common zeros\n   Given a sparse k-CNF F with at most Bn clauses, where B is polylogarithmic in n, we construct\nO(n) sparse polynomial equations on its original n variables whose common zeros are exactly its\nsatisfying assignments. One equation per clause could require Bn equations. Instead, each equation\nwill test the parity of the violations of a short list of clauses. We use Theorem 2.2 both to choose\nthese equations and to obtain the final count.\n   Let m be the number of indexed clauses and ve (x) the violation bit of clause e. As a polynomial\nover F2 , ve is a product of the factors 1 + xv for positive literals and xv for negative literals. A\ntautological clause has ve = 0 and an empty clause has ve = 1. After removing repeated literals,\ndeg ve ⩽ k and ve expands to at most 2k monomials. Put v(x) = (v1 (x), . . . , vm (x)). For a binary\nmatrix A with m columns, row i specifies the equation\n                                            X\n                                 Pi (x) :=       ve (x) = 0      over F2 .                       (6.1)\n                                          e:Aie =1\n\f40                                                OPENAI\n\n\nWrite PA for this system and N (A) = N (PA ) for its number of common zeros. Every satisfying\nassignment passes every row. Hence N (A) = #F + D(A), where D(A) counts the nonsatisfying\nassignments still passing. We do not know #F , but it is the same for every candidate next row: the\nrow minimizing N also minimizes D.\n   We represent a test row by a list of pairs (e, c) with e ∈ [m] and c ∈ {0, 1}, summing coefficients\nmodulo 2 if a clause index is repeated. The length of this list counts all pairs, including repeated\nindices and pairs with coefficient zero. A row of list length s thus reads at most ks variables before\ncancellation.\n6.1. Initial tests that exclude small violation sets. If an assignment violates only one of many\nclauses, a test sampling a short list of clauses is unlikely to notice it. We therefore first construct\na matrix A(0) that rejects every nonzero violation vector with small support. Every nonsatisfying\nassignment that passes these initial tests must violate many clauses. A further test that samples a\nshort list of clauses can then reject each such assignment with constant probability.\n   We use the standard edge-based Tanner code: bits are assigned to the edges of an expander, and\nthe bits incident to each vertex must form a word of a prescribed local linear code (see Tanner [Tan81,\nSection II] for the local constraints and Sipser and Spielman [SS96, Sections IV and VI] for the\nexpander construction). The usual local-distance and expansion argument gives the needed lower\nbound on the weight of a nonzero codeword. The proof below constructs the graph and local code\ndeterministically, with parameters that meet our bounds on the number and length of the tests.\nLemma 6.1. Let n, m, B be positive integers with m ⩽ Bn, and put\n                   µ = ⌊log2 m⌋ + 1,        P = 20000B 2 µ2 ,      D0 = 320000B 2 µ3 .\nThere is a deterministic construction of a binary matrix A(0) with m columns and at m