eger,\nand let L be the total length of the graph and tensor descriptions of a bipartite tensor network with at\nmost Cs edges and maximum degree at most ⌈s9/10 ⌉. Suppose that the number of vertices is bounded\nby a fixed polynomial in s, and that the entries belong to\n                           BP0 ,ℓ = (Z/2P0 Z)[V ]/(V ℓ ),     1 ⩽ P0 , ℓ ⩽ Cs.\nSuppose, uniformly for every vertex and Boolean choice of its ports, that its entry can be computed\n                                          9/10\nfrom the description in time (s + L)b 2As      and space (s + L)b . Then the contraction can be computed\ndeterministically in                                       √\n                                         (s + L)O(1) 2O(s/ log s)\nbit operations and (s + L)O(1) bits of space. Constants depend only on the fixed bounds in the\nhypotheses. In particular, ℓ = 1 is permitted.\n   The algorithm in Section D.4 replaces a single global Taylor expansion by recursion on the wires.\nAt each call it deforms the wire identifications so that, at a chosen interpolation node, only ports in\nthe same group interact. Near that node, interactions between groups expand in variables called\nmodes. A mode of weight j has coefficient divisible by 2j , so only monomials of total weight below\nthe working precision can contribute. Tapering the Hermite multiplicities with the two-adic valuation\nof the node bounds that precision.\n                              √        With M wires and u retained valuation levels, the number\nof mode monomials is 2O(M/ u) , and each monomial factors into contractions with at most M/8\n                                           −d                       d\n                      √ d, M = O(s8 ) and u = Θ((log s)/2 ). The exponents therefore sum\nwires. At recursion depth\ngeometrically to O(s/ log s).\n\f54                                                 OPENAI\n\n\nD.2. Fixed clause density.\nProof of the fixed-density bound in Theorem 1.1. Give each clause a vertex carrying its Boolean\nsatisfaction table, with one port for each distinct variable in the clause. A tautology may be dropped,\nand an empty clause gives count zero. For a variable with d ⩾ 1 ports, require their bits to agree.\nFor d = 1 attach the unary tensor (1, 1), and for d = 2 attach a binary equality tensor. For d ⩾ 3\ntake a tree with d external ports and trivalent equality tensors at its internal vertices. Such a tree\nhas d − 2 internal vertices, and each common value on its ports extends uniquely to all its internal\nedges.\n   There are at most km clause ports. The graph has Ok (n + m) edges and vertices, and maximum\ndegree at most max{k, 3}. Subdivide every edge once by a binary equality tensor. The old vertices\nand the new vertices form the two sides of a bipartite network. The two bits on a subdivided edge\nmust be equal, so this preserves the contraction. Every satisfying assignment on the occurring\nvariables contributes one and every other assignment contributes zero.\n   With s = n + m, apply Theorem D.1 over Z/2n+1 Z, taking P0 = n + 1 and ℓ = 1. The local\ntables have bounded size for fixed k. The degree and precision bounds hold for all sufficiently large\ns, and bounded instances can be enumerated directly. If u declared variables are unused, multiply\nthe contraction by 2u modulo 2n+1 . Its least nonnegative residue is #F , since 0 ⩽ #F ⩽ 2n .\nThis includes  √ the empty formula, for which the empty network has contraction one. The time is\nsOk (1) 2Ok (s/ log s) √\n                       and the space is polynomial. When m ⩽ Dn, we have n ⩽ s ⩽ (1 + D)n, giving\nexponent Ok,D (n/ log n).                                                                            □\nD.3. Arbitrary clause density. The sparsification and greedy row selection in Section 6 reduce\nthis case to counting common zeros of sparse polynomial equations. We retain the parity lift of\nSection 5. Its degree bound lets us encode a monomial by a subset of test–variable in