-2_44](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-47054-2_44). [Google](https://www.google.com/search?q=Fraire-Huacuja+%22Integer+linear+programming+formulation+and+exact+algorithm+for+computing+pathwidth%22+filetype%3Apdf). Already listed in `pathwidth_solvers_README.md` §1; repeated here because its B&B may carry a lower bound.\n467:- Lin, L. & Lin, Y. (2025). Discrete isoperimetric method for bandwidth, pathwidth and treewidth of hypercubes. *Discrete Appl. Math.* 363, 201–214. [doi:10.1016/j.dam.2024.12.001](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.dam.2024.12.001). [Google](https://www.google.com/search?q=Lin+Lin+%22Discrete+isoperimetric+method+for+bandwidth%2C+pathwidth+and+treewidth+of+hypercubes%22+filetype%3Apdf). Already listed under the paper-2 survey; the most recent isoperimetric pathwidth paper.\n489:  inequality (7.14) gives the lower bound 10 on Myciel4 (23 vertices), our exact\n503:bandwidth or cutwidth of μ(G) or of the Mycielski graphs: **no paper found**\n505:unknown. Exact values exist only as benchmark rows: Tamaki (2017/2019,\n516:  lower bounds for treewidth, pathwidth and carving width, the exact pathwidth of\n550:  blocked). Its first-moment bound `i_E(d) ≥ d/2 − √(d ln 2)` and its exact","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012BTJSuYjLWyc1XFPLWmjx5","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
