{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XbpFkFKWBfXuNv2EjVNPWF","type":"tool_result","content":"2905\t                Appendix C. Additional Consequences of Refuting ETH\n2906\t   Theorem 1.1 gives a circuit lower bound and subexponential-time algorithms for inverting Boolean\n2907\tmaps with constant output locality. The fixed-density case gives the quantitative circuit lower bound\n2908\tin Corollary 1.4. Both circuit arguments use only the decision version of our 3-SAT algorithm. Exact\n2909\tpreimage counting uses its full counting guarantee.\n2910\tC.1. Circuit lower bounds for ENP . For the qualitative bound below, write SIZE(O(n)) for the\n2911\tlanguages having nonuniform circuits with O(n) bounded-fan-in AND, OR, and NOT gates.\n2912\tCorollary C.1. We have\n2913\t                                         ENP ̸⊆ SIZE(O(n)).\n2914\tProof. Williams proved that if ETH is false, then ENP does not have linear-size circuits [Wil13,\n2915\tTheorem 6.1]. Apply this implication to the deterministic subexponential-time 3-SAT algorithm\n2916\tsupplied by Theorem 1.1.                                                                      □\n2917\t   The connection is an instance of deriving circuit lower bounds from faster satisfiability algorithms.\n2918\tTo encode a Boolean circuit with s gates and n inputs, introduce a variable for each gate, enforce\n2919\tits value from its inputs, and add a unit clause requiring the output bit to equal 1. The resulting\n2920\t3-CNF has O(n + s) variables and clauses and is satisfiable exactly when the circuit is. Our theorem\n2921\ttherefore decides satisfiability of linear-size circuits in 2o(n) time. Williams’s argument combines\n2922\tsuch a speedup with hypothetical small circuits for witnesses to contradict a nondeterministic time\n2923\thierarchy. The corollary gives a language in ENP without a linear-size circuit family; it does not\n2924\tassert a superpolynomial circuit lower bound for NP.\n2925\tProof of Corollary 1.4. We use Williams’s witness-circuit argument [Wil13, Theorem 6.1] with the\n2926\tlocal reduction of Jahanjou, Miles, and Viola [JMV13, Theorem 1 and Section 6].\n2927\t   By Žák’s nondeterministic time hierarchy [Žák83], in the form stated in [Wil13, Theorem 2.1\n2928\tin the full version], fix a unary language H ∈ NTIME(2n ) \\ NTIME(2n/2 ) on multitape Turing\n2929\tmachines. For all sufficiently large n apply the local reduction to its fixed machine on 1n with\n2930\tlocality parameter r = 2. A time allowance T = 2n nO(1) includes the polylogarithmic overhead of\n2931\tsimulating the machine in the reduction and gives q = n + O(log n) clause-index bits. In polynomial\n2932\ttime the reduction constructs a decoder giving three variable labels and their signs on each index.\n2933\tLet φn consist of the clauses decoded on all 2q indices. It is satisfiable exactly when 1n ∈ H.\n2934\t   Each of the decoder’s O(n) output bits has a constant-depth decision tree, so it depends on at\n2935\tmost a fixed number w of index bits. Evaluating the decoder on all strings of Hamming weight at\n2936\tmost w recovers each support. Indeed, two strings witnessing dependence on one coordinate can be\n2937\t\f52                                                    OPENAI\n2938\t\n2939\t\n2940\tset to zero off the support while retaining their differing outputs. The same evaluations give the\n2941\ttruth table on each support. This constructs a decoder of O(n) fan-in-two gates in polynomial time.\n2942\t    Pad each variable label to dn bits for a fixed positive integer d and declare all 2dn variables. If\n2943\t|i| = dn for such an n and φn is satisfiable, let W (i) be bit i of its lexicographically first satisfying\n2944\tassignment, ordering variables by their labels. Set W (i) = 0 in all other cases. The length of i\n2945\tdetermines n. To decide W , write out the 2q clauses, ask an NP oracle whether the formula is\n2946\tsatisfiable, and, if so, recover the first assignment by asking whether its current prefix followed by\n2947\tzero has a satisfying\n2948\t                p       extension. At most 2dn prefix queries of length 2O(n) suffice, so W ∈ ENP .\n2949\t    Put a(t) = log2 t. Testing whether the count in the constant-density part of Theorem 1.1 is\n2950\tpositive decides 3-SAT with V variables and at most 5V clauses in time V b 2AV /a(V ) for fixed A, b > 0.\n2951\tWe absorb the change of logarithm base in A. The solver uses polynomial space, so a multitape\n2952\tsimulation can scan its workspace for each bit access at a cost V O(1) , absorbed in b.\n2953\t    Fix a positive rational η < 1/(12A) and put sn = ⌊ηn⌊a(n)⌋⌋ for n ⩾ 2. The threshold is\n2954\tcomputable using the binary length of n and the integer square root of ⌊log2 n⌋. Suppose that\n2955\tCW (dn) ⩽ sn for all sufficiently large n. To decide H, reject nonunary inputs. On 1n , guess a circuit\n2956\ton dn index bits with at most sn gates. Feed the decoder’s three variable labels to three copies of\n2957\tthis circuit and test whether the indexed clause is violated. Accept if the test circuit is unsatisfiable.\n2958\tWhen 1n ∈ H, a circuit computing W at length dn gives an accepting guess. When 1n ∈            / H, every\n2959\tguess violates a clause.\n2960\t    The test circuit has q inputs and at most 3sn + O(n) gates. The encoding above uses one variable\n2961\tper input and gate, at most four clauses of width at most three per gate to enforce its truth table,\n2962\tand one unit clause to pin the output. Padding with unused variables gives Un = 3sn + Bn variables\n2963\tand at most 5Un clauses for a fixed integer B. Since Un = (3η + o(1))na(n) and a(Un ) ∼ a(n),\n2964\tthe test takes 2(3Aη+o(1))n time. Guessing and building the circuits take polynomial time, so H is\n2965\tdecidable nondeterministically in time 2n/2 for all sufficiently large n. The finitely many exceptional\n2966\tlengths can bephardwired, contradicting the choice of H. Thus CW (dn) >√        sn for infinitely many n.\n2967\tSince sn ∼ ηn log2 n and d is fixed, taking L = W and any 0 < c < η/(d log 2) proves (1.2). □\n2968\tC.2. Inverting Boolean maps with constant output locality. Fix a constant d. Consider a\n2969\tBoolean map G : {0, 1}n → {0, 1}m whose ith output bit has the form\n2970\t                              G(⃗x)i = gi (⃗x|Si ),      Si ⊆ [n],   |Si | ⩽ d.\n2971\tThe input description of G lists the sets Si and the truth tables of the Boolean functions gi . Thus\n2972\teach output inspects only constantly many input bits, although the sets Si may overlap arbitrarily.\n2973\tGiven a target ⃗b ∈ {0, 1}m , inversion asks for any ⃗x with G(⃗x) = ⃗b.\n2974\tCorollary C.2. For every fixed d and n ⩾ 20, the number |G−1 (⃗b)| can be computed exactly, and a\n2975\tpreimage can be found whenever one exists, in deterministic time\n2976\t                                          (n + m)Od (1) 2Od (ρ(n))\n2977\tand space (n + m)Od (1) .\n2978\t                                            α ∈ {0, 1}Si with gi (⃗\n2979\tProof. For each i and each local assignment ⃗                     α) ̸= bi , include the clause\n2980\t                               _\n2981\t                                   ℓj,αj ,  ℓj,0 = Xj , ℓj,1 = ¬Xj .\n2982\t                                j∈Si\n2983\t\n2984\tThis clause forbids exactly the local assignment ⃗  α. If Si is empty, its constraint is constant and\n2985\tcan be checked directly. The conjunction of the remaining clauses is a CNF on the original n\n2986\tdeclared variables, with width at most d and at most 2d m clauses. Its satisfying assignments are\n2987\texactly G−1 (⃗b); no auxiliary variables or multiplicity corrections are needed. Apply Theorem 1.1\n2988\twith k = max{2, d}. The usual variable-by-variable self-reduction finds a preimage using at most n\n2989\t\f                            THE EXPONENTIAL TIME HYPOTHESIS IS FALSE                                   53\n2990\t\n2991\t\n2992\tfurther counting queries, which only changes the polynomial factor. Each query may be discarded\n2993\tbefore the next, preserving polynomial space.                                                 □\n2994\t   Conditional counts also allow an exactly uniform preimage to be sampled, when the preimage set\n2995\tis nonempty, with the same bound in expected time up to polynomial factors [JVV86]. In particular,\n2996\tthe corollary applies to constant-locality versions of Goldreich’s candidate one-way functions, which\n2997\tevaluate local predicates on overlapping subsets of input bits [Gol00]. For polynomially many output\n2998\tbits, it gives a 2o(n) -time inversion algorithm for every output in the image, ruling out 2Ω(n) inversion\n2999\thardness for these families. This does not rule out one-wayness against polynomial-time algorithms.\n3000\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9be64548-7b53-4f99-81aa-4ed97f3b0de3","uuid":"d14c2a04-027e-48e4-8b1b-86e5f8ac2c94","timestamp":"2026-11-04T18:08:48.782Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/01_audit/eth.txt","content":"                Appendix C. Additional Consequences of Refuting ETH\n   Theorem 1.1 gives a circuit lower bound and subexponential-time algorithms for inverting Boolean\nmaps with constant output locality. The fixed-density case gives the quantitative circuit lower bound\nin Corollary 1.4. Both circuit arguments use only the decision version of our 3-SAT algorithm. Exact\npreimage counting uses its full counting guarantee.\nC.1. Circuit lower bounds for ENP . For the qualitative bound below, write SIZE(O(n)) for the\nlanguages having nonuniform circuits with O(n) bounded-fan-in AND, OR, and NOT gates.\nCorollary C.1. We have\n                                         ENP ̸⊆ SIZE(O(n)).\nProof. Williams proved that if ETH is false, then ENP does not have linear-size circuits [Wil13,\nTheorem 6.1]. Apply this implication to the deterministic subexponential-time 3-SAT algorithm\nsupplied by Theorem 1.1.                                                                      □\n   The connection is an instance of deriving circuit lower bounds from faster satisfiability algorithms.\nTo encode a Boolean circuit with s gates and n inputs, introduce a variable for each gate, enforce\nits value from its inputs, and add a unit clause requiring the output bit to equal 1. The resulting\n3-CNF has O(n + s) variables and clauses and is satisfiable exactly when the circuit is. Our theorem\ntherefore decides satisfiability of linear-size circuits in 2o(n) time. Williams’s argument combines\nsuch a speedup with hypothetical small circuits for witnesses to contradict a nondeterministic time\nhierarchy. The corollary gives a language in ENP without a linear-size circuit family; it does not\nassert a superpolynomial circuit lower bound for NP.\nProof of Corollary 1.4. We use Williams’s witness-circuit argument [Wil13, Theorem 6.1] with the\nlocal reduction of Jahanjou, Miles, and Viola [JMV13, Theorem 1 and Section 6].\n   By Žák’s nondeterministic time hierarchy [Žák83], in the form stated in [Wil13, Theorem 2.1\nin the full version], fix a unary language H ∈ NTIME(2n ) \\ NTIME(2n/2 ) on multitape Turing\nmachines. For all sufficiently large n apply the local reduction to its fixed machine on 1n with\nlocality parameter r = 2. A time allowance T = 2n nO(1) includes the polylogarithmic overhead of\nsimulating the machine in the reduction and gives q = n + O(log n) clause-index bits. In polynomial\ntime the reduction constructs a decoder giving three variable labels and their signs on each index.\nLet φn consist of the clauses decoded on all 2q indices. It is satisfiable exactly when 1n ∈ H.\n   Each of the decoder’s O(n) output bits has a constant-depth decision tree, so it depends on at\nmost a fixed number w of index bits. Evaluating the decoder on all strings of Hamming weight at\nmost w recovers each support. Indeed, two strings witnessing dependence on one coordinate can be\n\f52                                                    OPENAI\n\n\nset to zero off the support while retaining their differing outputs. The same evaluations give the\ntruth table on each support. This constructs a decoder of O(n) fan-in-two gates in polynomial time.\n    Pad each variable label to dn bits for a fixed positive integer d and declare all 2dn variables. If\n|i| = dn for such an n and φn is satisfiable, let W (i) be bit i of its lexicographically first satisfying\nassignment, ordering variables by their labels. Set W (i) = 0 in all other cases. The length of i\ndetermines n. To decide W , write out the 2q clauses, ask an NP oracle whether the formula is\nsatisfiable, and, if so, recover the first assignment by asking whether its current prefix followed by\nzero has a satisfying\n                p       extension. At most 2dn prefix queries of length 2O(n) suffice, so W ∈ ENP .\n    Put a(t) = log2 t. Testing whether the count in the constant-density part of Theorem 1.1 is\npositive decides 3-SAT with V variables and at most 5V clauses in time V b 2AV /a(V ) for fixed A, b > 0.\nWe absorb the change of logarithm base in A. The solver uses polynomial space, so a multitape\nsimulation can scan its workspace for each bit access at a cost V O(1) , absorbed in b.\n    Fix a positive rational η < 1/(12A) and put sn = ⌊ηn⌊a(n)⌋⌋ for n ⩾ 2. The threshold is\ncomputable using the binary length of n and the integer square root of ⌊log2 n⌋. Suppose that\nCW (dn) ⩽ sn for all sufficiently large n. To decide H, reject nonunary inputs. On 1n , guess a circuit\non dn index bits with at most sn gates. Feed the decoder’s three variable labels to three copies of\nthis circuit and test whether the indexed clause is violated. Accept if the test circuit is unsatisfiable.\nWhen 1n ∈ H, a circuit computing W at length dn gives an accepting guess. When 1n ∈            / H, every\nguess violates a clause.\n    The test circuit has q inputs and at most 3sn + O(n) gates. The encoding above uses one variable\nper input and gate, at most four clauses of width at most three per gate to enforce its truth table,\nand one unit clause to pin the output. Padding with unused variables gives Un = 3sn + Bn variables\nand at most 5Un clauses for a fixed integer B. Since Un = (3η + o(1))na(n) and a(Un ) ∼ a(n),\nthe test takes 2(3Aη+o(1))n time. Guessing and building the circuits take polynomial time, so H is\ndecidable nondeterministically in time 2n/2 for all sufficiently large n. The finitely many exceptional\nlengths can bephardwired, contradicting the choice of H. Thus CW (dn) >√        sn for infinitely many n.\nSince sn ∼ ηn log2 n and d is fixed, taking L = W and any 0 < c < η/(d log 2) proves (1.2). □\nC.2. Inverting Boolean maps with constant output locality. Fix a constant d. Consider a\nBoolean map G : {0, 1}n → {0, 1}m whose ith output bit has the form\n                              G(⃗x)i = gi (⃗x|Si ),      Si ⊆ [n],   |Si | ⩽ d.\nThe input description of G lists the sets Si and the truth tables of the Boolean functions gi . Thus\neach output inspects only constantly many input bits, although the sets Si may overlap arbitrarily.\nGiven a target ⃗b ∈ {0, 1}m , inversion asks for any ⃗x with G(⃗x) = ⃗b.\nCorollary C.2. For every fixed d and n ⩾ 20, the number |G−1 (⃗b)| can be computed exactly, and a\npreimage can be found whenever one exists, in deterministic time\n                                          (n + m)Od (1) 2Od (ρ(n))\nand space (n + m)Od (1) .\n                                            α ∈ {0, 1}Si with gi (⃗\nProof. For each i and each local assignment ⃗                     α) ̸= bi , include the clause\n                               _\n                                   ℓj,αj ,  ℓj,0 = Xj , ℓj,1 = ¬Xj .\n                                j∈Si\n\nThis clause forbids exactly the local assignment ⃗  α. If Si is empty, its constraint is constant and\ncan be checked directly. The conjunction of the remaining clauses is a CNF on the original n\ndeclared variables, with width at most d and at most 2d m clauses. Its satisfying assignments are\nexactly G−1 (⃗b); no auxiliary variables or multiplicity corrections are needed. Apply Theorem 1.1\nwith k = max{2, d}. The usual variable-by-variable self-reduction finds a preimage using at most n\n\f                            THE EXPONENTIAL TIME HYPOTHESIS IS FALSE                                   53\n\n\nfurther counting queries, which only changes the polynomial factor. Each query may be discarded\nbefore the next, preserving polynomial space.                                                 □\n   Conditional counts also allow an exactly uniform preimage to be sampled, when the preimage set\nis nonempty, with the same bound in expected time up to polynomial factors [JVV86]. In particular,\nthe corollary applies to constant-locality versions of Goldreich’s candidate one-way functions, which\nevaluate local predicates on overlapping subsets of input bits [Gol00]. For polynomially many output\nbits, it gives a 2o(n) -time inversion algorithm for every output in the image, ruling out 2Ω(n) inversion\nhardness for these families. This does not rule out one-wayness against polynomial-time algorithms.\n","numLines":96,"startLine":2905,"totalLines":3815}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01XbpFkFKWBfXuNv2EjVNPWF","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
