{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01452xCQYzrv3zZ7XXUxwebh","type":"tool_result","content":"====OAI-PROOF\n/home/al/dev/untrusted/openai-math/lean/formalization.yaml\n/home/al/dev/untrusted/openai-math/lean/OAI/Algorithms/SubexponentialSAT/Main.lean\n/home/al/dev/untrusted/openai-math/lean/OAI/Algorithms/SubexponentialSAT/SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean\n/home/al/dev/untrusted/openai-math/lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.json\n/home/al/dev/untrusted/openai-math/lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean\n\n# The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False\n\nThe following describes the scope of the Lean formalization related to the following accompanying paper(s):\n\n- [The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False](../../preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf)\n\n## Scope\n\nFor fixed $k\\ge2$, Lean proves that a deterministic machine exactly counts satisfying assignments of a canonically encoded $k$-CNF on $n$ variables in time $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}2^{O_k(n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$, where $L$ is the encoding's bit length. At every step, the machine has visited at most $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}$ tape cells in total.\n\nFor $m$ clauses, the October 25 manuscript gives the sharper time bound $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$. When $m\\le Dn$ for fixed $D>0$, this improves to $(n+m)^{O_{k,D}(1)}2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$.\n\n## Comparator links\n\n| Result | Comparator statement |\n| --- | --- |\n| Exact counting and SAT on canonical inputs in subexponential time and polynomial space | [SubexponentialSAT.lean](../ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean) |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9be64548-7b53-4f99-81aa-4ed97f3b0de3","uuid":"e3bea8d9-c0bb-45e0-b36c-a5fe51b80c3b","timestamp":"2026-11-04T18:11:30.532Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"====OAI-PROOF\n/home/al/dev/untrusted/openai-math/lean/formalization.yaml\n/home/al/dev/untrusted/openai-math/lean/OAI/Algorithms/SubexponentialSAT/Main.lean\n/home/al/dev/untrusted/openai-math/lean/OAI/Algorithms/SubexponentialSAT/SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean\n/home/al/dev/untrusted/openai-math/lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.json\n/home/al/dev/untrusted/openai-math/lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean\n\n# The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False\n\nThe following describes the scope of the Lean formalization related to the following accompanying paper(s):\n\n- [The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False](../../preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf)\n\n## Scope\n\nFor fixed $k\\ge2$, Lean proves that a deterministic machine exactly counts satisfying assignments of a canonically encoded $k$-CNF on $n$ variables in time $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}2^{O_k(n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$, where $L$ is the encoding's bit length. At every step, the machine has visited at most $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}$ tape cells in total.\n\nFor $m$ clauses, the October 25 manuscript gives the sharper time bound $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$. When $m\\le Dn$ for fixed $D>0$, this improves to $(n+m)^{O_{k,D}(1)}2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$.\n\n## Comparator links\n\n| Result | Comparator statement |\n| --- | --- |\n| Exact counting and SAT on canonical inputs in subexponential time and polynomial space | [SubexponentialSAT.lean](../ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean) |","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01452xCQYzrv3zZ7XXUxwebh","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
