{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GVQLqSyjvzxqApaPHYVptS","type":"tool_result","content":"=== ITCS 820-840\nthis paper. Next, there are a lot more (vertex-ordering) problems that are also interesting to study.\nExamples include Treewidth, Subset Feedback Vertex/Arc Set (here also the restriction\n\n                                                  13\n\fto tournament graphs is often studied), Travelling Sales Person, Bandwidth, or Minimum\nFill-In. Second, can our constant-factor approximations be improved to (1 + ε) as in the case\nof Feedback Arc Set? On a related note, we are not aware of any tool to show lower bounds\nfor exponential-time approximations and most known hypotheses like SETH seem to weak to base\nsuch lower bounds on. In particular, a reasonably sounding candidate one could call Gap-SETH\n(a combination of SETH and Gap-ETH) is not true as shown by Alman et al. [1]. Hence, we ask\nthe following question:\n     What would be a sensible hypothesis to exclude (1 + ε)-approximations in O((2 − δε )n )\n     time for some δε > 0 depending only on ε for different problems?\nFinally, all our algorithms use exponential space (here, the bottleneck is our exact algorithm for\nDirected Minimum (k, n − k)-Cut, which first builds an exponential-size graph). Can this be\nreduced to polynomial space while still beating the fastest known exact algorithms?\n\n\nReferences\n=== ITCS refs\n841: [1] Josh Alman, Timothy M. Chan, and R. Ryan Williams. Faster deterministic and Las Vegas\n879:[12] Marek Cygan and Marcin Pilipczuk. Exact and approximate bandwidth. Theoretical Com-\n881:[13] Marek Cygan, Daniel Lokshtanov, Marcin Pilipczuk, Michal Pilipczuk, and Saket Saurabh.\n904:[21] Fedor V. Fomin and Dieter Kratsch. Exact Exponential Algorithms. Springer, 2010.\n910:[24] Thore Husfeldt, Dieter Kratsch, Ramamohan Paturi, and Gregory B. Sorkin, editors. Ex-\n913:[25] Thore Husfeldt, Ramamohan Paturi, Gregory B. Sorkin, and Ryan Williams, editors. Ex-\n928:[28] Pallavi Jain, Lawqueen Kanesh, William Lochet, Saket Saurabh, and Roohani Sharma. Exact\n943:[33] Jesper Nederlof. Bipartite TSP in o(1.9999n ) time, assuming quadratic time matrix multipli-\n946:[34] Jesper Nederlof, Jakub Pawlewicz, Céline M. F. Swennenhuis, and Karol Wegrzycki. A faster\n954:[37] Or Zamir. Breaking the 2n barrier for 5-coloring and 6-coloring. In Procceedings of the\n[12] Marek Cygan and Marcin Pilipczuk. Exact and approximate bandwidth. Theoretical Com-\n     puter Science, 411(40-42):3701–3713, 2010.\n[13] Marek Cygan, Daniel Lokshtanov, Marcin Pilipczuk, Michal Pilipczuk, and Saket Saurabh.\n     On cutwidth parameterized by vertex cover. Algorithmica, 68(4):940–953, 2014.\n[14] Irit Dinur and Samuel Safra. On the hardness of approximating minimum vertex cover.\n[28] Pallavi Jain, Lawqueen Kanesh, William Lochet, Saket Saurabh, and Roohani Sharma. Exact\n     and approximate digraph bandwidth. In Proceedings of the 39th IARCS Annual Conference\n     on Foundations of Software Technology and Theoretical Computer Science (FSTTCS), pages\n     18:1–18:15. Schloss Dagstuhl - Leibniz-Zentrum für Informatik, 2019.\n=== SV refs 11 16\n560:11. M. Habib and R. H. Mhring, Treewidth of cocomparability graphs and a new\n572:16. R. Mihai and I. Todinca, Pathwidth is np-hard for weighted trees, in FAW,\n11. M. Habib and R. H. Mhring, Treewidth of cocomparability graphs and a new\n    order-theoretic parameter, Order, 11 (1994), pp. 47–60.\n12. P. Heggernes, K. Suchan, I. Todinca, and Y. Villanger, Characterizing\n16. R. Mihai and I. Todinca, Pathwidth is np-hard for weighted trees, in FAW,\n    X. Deng, J. E. Hopcroft, and J. Xue, eds., vol. 5598 of Lecture Notes in Computer\n    Science, Springer, 2009, pp. 181–195.\n=== survey ref 15 and 47\n706:15. Édouard Bonnet: Treewidth inapproximability and tight ETH lower bound (2024).\n802:47. Wu, Y.L., Austrin, P., Pitassi, T., Liu, D.: Inapproximability of treewidth and\n15. Édou