{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01U4MFJzZmTcyWSRbbEMHHh9","type":"tool_result","content":"31\t                                               1. Introduction\n32\t   For every fixed k ⩾ 3, k-SAT is the canonical NP-complete problem [Coo71], and its exponential-\n33\ttime complexity underlies the Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH) and the Strong Exponential\n34\tTime Hypothesis (SETH) [IP01, IPZ01]. In this paper, we refute ETH by giving, for every fixed\n35\tk ⩾ 2, a deterministic algorithm that counts the satisfying assignments of a k-CNF in subexponential\n36\ttime and polynomial space.\n37\t   Write the input k-CNF on n declared variables with m input clauses as\n38\t                                                m\n39\t                                                ^ \u0002                                         \u0003\n40\t                         F (x1 , . . . , xn ) =    (xie,1 ⊕ be,1 ) ∨ · · · ∨ (xie,k ⊕ be,k ) ,\n41\t                                              e=1\n42\t\n43\twhere ie,j ∈ {1, . . . , n} and be,j ∈ {0, 1} are the variable index and negation bit of the jth literal in\n44\tclause e, and ⊕ denotes addition modulo two. Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than k\n45\tliterals. The indices ie,j and bits be,j are fixed by F ; only x1 , . . . , xn vary over assignments. Our aim\n46\tis to compute the quantity\n47\t                                      #F = {x ∈ {0, 1}n : F (x) = 1} .                                   (1.1)\n48\tTesting #F > 0 also decides satisfiability. We keep k fixed as n grows. Throughout, log denotes the\n49\tnatural logarithm.\n50\t\n51\t  Date: 17 October 2026, revised 25 October 2026.\n52\t                                                          1\n53\t\f2                                                  OPENAI\n54\t\n55\t\n56\tTheorem 1.1. For each fixed k ⩾ 2, there exists a deterministic algorithm that, given a k-CNF F\n57\ton n ⩾ 20 declared variables with m clauses, computes #F as defined in (1.1) in\n58\t                                                                  √\n59\t                                    (n + m)Ok (1) 2Ok (n log log n/ log n)\n60\tbit operations and (n + m)Ok (1) bits of space. For each fixed D > 0, its running time on inputs with\n61\tm ⩽ Dn is                                                     √\n62\t                                     (n + m)Ok,D (1) 2Ok,D (n/ log n) .\n63\t    The rational interpolation argument in Section 6.4 gives the weaker exponent Ok (ρ(n)), where\n64\t                                           n(log log n)2         n\n65\t                                  ρ(n) =      √          =         1       .\n66\t                                                log n      (log n) 2 +o(1)\n67\tAppendix D proves both bounds in Theorem 1.1 by tensor contraction.\n68\t   When #F > 0, the standard self-reduction finds a satisfying assignment by fixing one variable at\n69\ta time and checking that the remaining count is positive. Choosing each bit according to the two\n70\tconditional counts instead gives an exactly uniform random satisfying assignment, with polyk (n, m)\n71\texpected overhead [JVV86, Theorem 3.3(1)].\n72\t   The theorem also yields arbitrarily accurate subexponential-time approximation algorithms for\n73\tMax-k-SAT. The Unique Games Theorem [Ope26, Section 8.1] implies NP-hardness bounds for\n74\tMax-k-SAT that match, up to arbitrarily small error in the approximation ratio, the guarantees of\n75\tthe semidefinite-programming algorithm of [Rag08]. Write OPT(F ) for the maximum number of\n76\tclauses of F simultaneously satisfied by an assignment.\n77\tCorollary 1.2. For every fixed k ⩾ 2 and ε > 0, there exists a randomized algorithm that, given a\n78\tk-CNF F on n ⩾ 20 variables with m clauses, finds an assignment satisfying at least OPT(F ) − εm\n79\tclauses with probability 1 − 2−Ω(n) , in\n80\t                                        (n + m)Ok,ε (1) 2Ok,ε (ρ(n))\n81\ttime and (n + m)Ok,ε (1) space.\n82\tProof. We may assume m > 0. Let f (x) be the fraction of clauses satisfied by x, and let fb(x) be\n83\tthat fraction in a sample of O(n/ε2 ) clauses chosen independently and uniformly with replacement.\n84\tHoeffding’s inequality and a union bound over all 2n assignments give, with probability 1 − 2−Ω(n) ,\n85\t                           |fb(x) − f (x)| ⩽ ε/2    for every x ∈ {0, 1}n .\n86\tThus, if x                       x) ⩾ maxx f (x) − ε. Given an integer threshold b, a Boolean circuit\n87\t         b maximizes fb, then f (b\n88\twith at most two inputs per gate and size Ok,ε (n) tests whether an assignment satisfies at least b\n89\tsampled clauses. The standard circuit-to-3-CNF reduction [Bar23, Lemmas 15.9–15.10] produces\n90\ta formula with Ok,ε (n) variables and clauses that is satisfiable exactly when this threshold can be\n91\tattained. Theorem 1.1, binary search on the threshold, and self-reduction then find an exact sample\n92\toptimizer within the stated bounds. Truncating rejection sampling after O(n + log(n/ε2 )) trials\n93\tper sample gives worst-case poly(n, m, ε−1 ) sampling time and adds at most 2−Ω(n) to the failure\n94\tprobability.                                                                                       □\n95\t   Note that runtime bounds of the form 2o(n) do not transfer under general polynomial-time\n96\t              o(1)\n97\treductions (2n     would be needed for this), so our result does not imply that general NP problems\n98\tadmit subexponential time algorithms. However we do obtain subexponential time algorithms for\n99\tcounting graph colorings and perfect matchings.\n100\tCorollary 1.3. On simple graphs with v vertices and e edges, the number of independent sets and,\n101\tfor each fixed q ⩾ 2, the number of proper q-colorings can be computed deterministically in 2o(v) time\n102\tand polyq (v, e) space. The number of perfect matchings can be computed deterministically in 2o(v+e)\n103\ttime and poly(v, e) space.\n104\t\f                           THE EXPONENTIAL TIME HYPOTHESIS IS FALSE                                 3\n105\t\n106\t\n107\tProof. For G = (V, E), introduce a Boolean variable xu for each u ∈ V . Satisfying assignments of\n108\t                                        ^\n109\t                                             (¬xu ∨ ¬xw )\n110\t                                        {u,w}∈E\n111\t\n112\tcorrespond bijectively to the independent sets {u ∈ V : xu = 1}. For proper q-colorings, use one\n113\tBoolean variable per vertex and color, require exactly one color at each vertex, and forbid equal\n114\tcolors at adjacent vertices. This gives a counting-preserving q-CNF encoding with qv variables and\n115\tOq (v + e) clauses.\n116\t   For perfect matchings, use one Boolean variable per edge and a linear-size circuit checking that\n117\texactly one selected edge meets each vertex. The standard circuit-to-3-CNF reduction [Bar23,\n118\tLemmas 15.9–15.10] gives O(v + e) variables and clauses. The gate variables have unique values\n119\tonce the edge bits are fixed, so this encoding also preserves the count. Theorem 1.1 gives the stated\n120\ttime and space bounds.                                                                             □\n121\t  The fixed-density case of Theorem 1.1 gives a circuit lower bound for ENP , the class of languages\n122\tdecidable in deterministic time 2O(n) with an NP oracle.\n123\tCorollary 1.4. Let CL (n) be the minimum number of noninput gates in a circuit computing L on\n124\t{0, 1}n , allowing arbitrary Boolean gates of fan-in at most two and unrestricted fan-out. There exist\n125\ta language L ∈ ENP and a constant c > 0 such that\n126\t                                         p\n127\t                             CL (n) > c n log n      for infinitely many n.                      (1.2)\n128\t  Appendix C.1 proves the corollary. Appendix C.2 gives algorithms for inversion and exact preimage\n129\tcounting of Boolean maps with constant output locality.\n130\t1.1. Related work. We first discuss earlier SAT and counting algorithms and the complexity\n131\thypotheses contradicted by Theorem 1.1. We then compare our approach with earlier algebraic\n132\talgorithms and identify the existing constructions used in our proof, whose roles are developed in\n133\tthe roadmap in Section 2.\n134\t1.1.1. SAT algorithms and the exponential-time hypotheses. Schöning’s local-search algorithm and\n135\tPPSZ are landmarks in the development of exponential-time algorithms for k-SAT [Sch99, PPSZ05].\n136\tSchöning’s randomized algorithm starts from a random assignment and repeatedly flips a uniformly\n137\tchosen variable in an unsatisfied clause. Its bounded-error decision time is\n138\t                                       (n + m)O(1) (2 − 2/k)n\n139\tfor each fixed k ⩾ 3 [Sch99, Section 2]. In particular, this gives (n + m)O(1) (4/3)n time for 3-SAT.\n140\tThe PPSZ algorithm [PPSZ05] uses a different mechanism: bounded resolution derives additional\n141\tclauses, after which variables are assigned in a random order, taking forced values when available and\n142\tguessing otherwise. For k = 3, 4, its original analysis gave stronger bounds for uniquely satisfiable\n143\tformulas than for general ones. The unique-SAT guarantees were later extended to general 3-SAT\n144\tand 4-SAT; for 3-SAT this gave a randomized O(1.308n ) bound [Her14]. Hansen, Kaplan, Zamir, and\n145\tZwick improved the PPSZ bound for every fixed k ⩾ 3 by biasing the algorithm’s guesses [HKZZ19].\n146\tScheder obtained an improvement for the same range of k by sharpening the analysis of the original\n147\tPPSZ algorithm [Sch24, Theorem 1.5]. For general 3-SAT, a preprint by Jiang and Cai gives a\n148\trandomized O∗ (1.307031578n ) bound [JC26, Corollary 1.2], where O∗ suppresses factors polynomial\n149\tin the input size. Liu’s deterministic algorithm combines branching with derandomized local search\n150\tand solves 3-SAT in O(1.32793n ) time [Liu18, Theorem 21].\n151\t   Randomized local search has also produced effective heuristics. The WalkSAT heuristic chooses an\n152\tunsatisfied clause and flips one of its variables, combining random choices with greedy moves [SKC94].\n153\tExperiments on formulas from circuit synthesis and diagnosis demonstrated the practical value of\n154\tthis approach [SKC94, pp. 340–342, Tables 4–5].\n155\t\f4                                                OPENAI\n156\t\n157\t\n158\t   Among uniformly random k-CNFs, instances at the satisfiability threshold were conjectured\n159\tto be the hardest for deciding satisfiability [VW21, Section 1]; for fixed k ⩾ 3 and lower clause\n160\tdensities m/n ⩽ 2k /k C , where C is a universal constant, polynomial-time almost-uniform sampling\n161\tof satisfying assignments is known with high probability over the formula [CLWYY25, Theorem 1.2].\n162\tFor a uniformly random formula with polynomially many clauses, conditioned on satisfiability, Vyas\n163\tand Williams [VW21, Theorem 3] give a PPZ-based algorithm that finds a solution in 2n(1−Ω(log k/k))\n164\ttime with high probability over both the formula and the algorithm’s random choices.\n165\t   The Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH) asserted that 3-SAT requires 2Ω(n) deterministic time\n166\tin the worst case, and hence so does k-SAT for every fixed k ⩾ 3 [IP01, Theorem 1]. The Strong\n167\tExponential Time Hypothesis (SETH) made the sharper prediction that k-SAT requires 2n(1−ok (1))\n168\ttime as k grows. Precisely, for every ε ∈ (0, 1), there is a fixed k for which no deterministic algorithm\n169\tsolves k-SAT in 2(1−ε)n (n + m)O(1) time. The running-time bounds for Schöning’s algorithm and\n170\tPPSZ helped motivate this prediction: their improvements over exhaustive search diminish as k\n171\tgrows [IP01, Open Problems, item 2].\n172\t   The Gap Exponential Time Hypothesis (Gap-ETH) strengthens ETH by postulating constants\n173\tδ, γ > 0 for which no O(2γn )-time algorithm can distinguish a satisfiable 3-CNF from one in which\n174\tevery assignment violates at least a δ fraction of the clauses, even when m = O(n) [Din16, MR17].\n175\tThe randomized version rules out an algorithm with the same running time that distinguishes\n176\tthe two cases with probability at least 2/3 on every promised input [CCK+ 20, Conjecture 2.5].\n177\tIt has been used to prove hardness of approximating Label Cover [Din16] and dense constraint\n178\tsatisfaction problems [MR17]. It also rules out nontrivial fixed-parameter approximations for Clique\n179\tand Dominating Set. For Clique, the excluded ratios are o(OPT), where OPT is the maximum\n180\tclique size [CCK+ 20, Corollaries 5.3 and 5.6].\n181\t   The Nondeterministic Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis (NSETH) asserts that, for every ε > 0,\n182\tsome fixed k admits no nondeterministic 2(1−ε)n (n + m)O(1) -time algorithm for deciding whether\n183\ta k-CNF is unsatisfiable, or equivalently whether a k-DNF is a tautology [CGI+ 16]. NSETH has\n184\tbeen used to establish barriers to deterministic fine-grained reductions from SAT to problems such\n185\tas 3SUM and All-Pairs Shortest Paths [CGI+ 16], and to delineate SETH-based lower bounds for\n186\tapproximating directed graph diameter [Li21].\n187\t   Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing whether the exact count\n188\tis positive or zero decides both satisfiability and unsatisfiability in deterministic 2o(n) (n+m)Ok (1) time\n189\tfor every fixed k; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise problem. The deterministic\n190\talgorithm also refutes the bounded-error randomized versions of ETH [DHM+ 14, Section 1] and\n191\tSETH [CDL+ 16, Definition 2.1], and of Gap-ETH. It does not conflict with unconditional exponential\n192\tlower bounds for restricted proof systems such as regular resolution [BI13]. In 2016, Ryan Williams\n193\trefuted the Merlin–Arthur version of SETH (MA-SETH) by giving a protocol that certifies k-CNF\n194\tunsatisfiability in 2n/2 poly(n, m) time [Wil16, Theorem 3.3].\n195\t\n196\t\n197\t1.1.2. Exact counting and counting complexity. In Valiant’s framework, #SAT and #3-SAT are\n198\t#P-complete under parsimonious reductions [Val79, Section 3, Facts 1–2]. For fixed k ⩾ 3, previous\n199\texact-counting algorithms achieve polyk (n, m)2n−Ω(n/k) time, with a Las Vegas expected-time\n200\tbound in [IMP12, Lemma 3.5] and a deterministic bound in [CW21, Theorem 1.6]. For #2-SAT,\n201\tWahlström’s algorithm counts satisfying assignments in O∗ (1.2377n ) time [Wah08]. For #3-SAT,\n202\tKutzkov uses complementary branching to count satisfying assignments in O∗ (1.637n ) time [Kut26].\n203\tBoth algorithms are deterministic.\n204\t   The counting Exponential Time Hypothesis (#ETH) asserted that exactly counting the satisfying\n205\tassignments of a 3-CNF requires 2Ω(n) deterministic time [DHM+ 14, Section 1]. Under #ETH,\n206\tCurticapean [Cur18] rules out deterministic 2o(v) -time algorithms for counting perfect matchings\n207\teven in unweighted bipartite graphs with v vertices and O(v) edges. The perfect-matching algorithm\n208\t\f                            THE EXPONENTIAL TIME HYPOTHESIS IS FALSE                                   5\n209\t\n210\t\n211\tin Corollary 1.3 applies in particular to these graphs. Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1) solves #3-SAT\n212\tdeterministically in 2o(n) poly(n, m) time and therefore refutes #ETH.\n213\t1.1.3. Implications for fine-grained complexity. ETH and SETH became standard assumptions for\n214\tparameterized and fine-grained complexity [IPZ01, VW19]. ETH-based reductions yielded lower\n215\tbounds for the dependence on solution size in Clique [CHKX06], and on treewidth in constraint\n216\tsatisfaction and partitioned subgraph isomorphism [Mar10]. SETH supported sharp exponential\n217\tbounds for Hitting Set and Set Splitting [CDL+ 16], and for Independent Set and Dominating Set\n218\tparameterized by treewidth [LMS18]. It also gave bounds on possible speedups of pseudopolynomial\n219\talgorithms for Subset Sum and Bicriteria Path [ABHS19].\n220\t   For problems in P, SETH was used to rule out polynomial-factor speedups over quadratic running\n221\ttimes for edit distance [BI18], longest common subsequence and dynamic time warping [BK15],\n222\tFréchet distance [Bri14], and regular-expression matching [BI16]. Other applications concerned\n223\tapproximating graph diameter [RV13] and maintaining reachability counts or the size of the largest\n224\tstrongly connected component under graph updates [AW14]. Together these results made SETH a\n225\tcommon basis for explaining the apparent optimality of algorithms across quite different problems.\n226\t   Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis for the hardness claims above. Some\n227\tof the same lower bounds follow from weaker hypotheses that may still hold. For example, the\n228\tlower bounds for longest common subsequence and regular-expression matching follow from the\n229\tOrthogonal Vectors Conjecture (OVC) [BK15, BI16]. OVC asserts that for every fixed ε > 0, no\n230\tO(N 2−ε poly(d))-time algorithm can decide whether two sets of N Boolean vectors of dimension\n231\td contain an orthogonal pair, one vector from each set. The reduction from SAT to Orthogonal\n232\tVectors shows that SETH implies OVC [Wil05, VW19]. The lower bounds for Fréchet distance,\n233\tgraph diameter approximation, and the treewidth dependence of Independent Set and Dominating\n234\tSet also follow from the hypothesis that unrestricted CNF-SAT admits no 2(1−δ)n poly(n, m)-time\n235\talgorithm for any constant δ > 0 [Bri14, RV13, LMS18]. Theorem 1.1 gives a running-time guarantee\n236\tfor each fixed clause width k, while this hypothesis allows the width to grow with n.\n237\t   To obtain a faster algorithm for a target problem from our SAT algorithm, we need a reduction in\n238\tthe other direction, from that problem to fixed-width SAT, that controls the number of variables.\n239\tSet Splitting with sets of size at most k has such a reduction [CDL+ 16, Observation 3.6]: use one\n240\tBoolean variable per element to specify its side of the split. For each set, one clause requires at least\n241\tone of its variables to be true, and a second requires at least one to be false. The resulting k-CNF\n242\thas one variable per universe element, so for fixed k Theorem 1.1 gives subexponential time in the\n243\tuniverse size, up to a polynomial input-size factor.\n244\t1.1.4. Prior work on algebraic algorithms for combinatorial problems. Our algorithm is algebraic,\n245\tbased on polynomial interpolation of carefully chosen auxiliary variables. Below we discuss prior work\n246\ton using algebraic methods to design algorithm for combinatorial problems. For broader background,\n247\tsee the surveys of Koutis and Williams [KW16] on algebraic fingerprints, Williams [Wil14] on the\n248\tpolynomial method in algorithm design, and Husfeldt [Hus11] on algorithmic inclusion–exclusion.\n249\t   Williams’s split-and-list algorithm [Wil05] partitions the variables of a 2-CSP into three groups,\n250\tenumerates partial assignments, and combines them using fast matrix multiplication. Koivisto [Koi06]\n251\tgives a direct sum–product formulation. The algorithm of Sections 3–6 likewise enumerates as-\n252\tsignments within groups. Fixing the shared measurements ⃗y makes their contributions multiply\n253\tindependently. Scott and Sorkin [SS09, Section 6] extend split-and-list to polynomial-valued weights\n254\tand arbitrary rings. Our summation routine similarly uses truncated polynomial rings to retain\n255\tseveral coefficients in one computation.\n256\t   Björklund [Bjö16, Sections 2–3] uses inclusion–exclusion and polynomial interpolation to reduce\n257\tpermanent and directed Hamiltonian-cycle counting to a weighted sum over smaller instances. He\n258\tadds the weights of identical instances in a table, then solves each distinct instance with nonzero\n259\ttotal weight once. Björklund, Kaski, and Williams [BKW19] use low-degree curves and generalized\n260\t\f6                                              OPENAI\n261\t\n262\t\n263\tKakeya sets to recover polynomial values from evaluations on a small structured set, yielding faster\n264\talgorithms for fermionants, including the permanent of integer matrices whose entries are bounded\n265\tin absolute value by a fixed constant. Our polynomial replacements serve a similar purpose: they\n266\tconnect the desired count to structured evaluations from which it can be reconstructed. Here the\n267\tuseful structure is separation into groups after fixing a few shared measurements.\n268\t   Bhargava, Ghosh, Kumar, and Mohapatra [BGKM23, Sections 3.1–3.2] evaluate multivariate\n269\tpolynomials over finite fields along low-degree curves, using Hasse derivatives and Hermite interpola-\n270\ttion when values alone do not suffice. For a univariate restriction g, the order-j Hasse derivative at\n271\tt is the coefficient of z j in g(t + z). We use the same recovery principle: compute several Taylor\n272\tcoefficients at each point admitting fast summation, then recover the original count by Hermite\n273\tinterpolation.\n274\t   Woodruff and Yekhanin [WY07] use values and first derivatives along a line to recover a target\n275\tvalue in private information retrieval. Kopparty, Saraf, and Yekhanin [KSY14] use higher-order\n276\tHasse derivatives for local correction of multiplicity codes, extending the line-based recovery of\n277\tReed–Muller codes. In our algorithm, the matrix construction supplies low-degree replacement forms\n278\tthat let us compute the Taylor coefficients at the interpolation points by fast summation after fixing\n279\ta few shared measurements.\n280\t1.1.5. Antecedents of the matrix construction. Our counting algorithm evaluates sums of products\n281\tof polynomials over Boolean assignments. The polynomials share variables, so these sums do not\n282\tfactor into independent smaller sums. We introduce linear substitutions, depending polynomially on\n283\tan interpolation parameter, that make such a factorization possible after fixing a few integer linear\n284\tmeasurements. An auxiliary square rational matrix M0 underlies these substitutions: a bound on\n285\trank(I − M0 ) limits the number of measurements, while restrictions on the nonzero entries of M0\n286\tkeep the substitution degrees small. Section 2.7 explains this connection.\n287\t   Related interpolation methods have been used to obtain low-rank approximations to structured\n288\tmatrices. In [AW17, Section 3], a low-degree polynomial matches (−1)j on an interval of integer\n289\tinner products j, using the interpolation lemma of [AW15, Lemma 3.1]. This gives low-rank\n290\tmatrices agreeing with Walsh–Hadamard matrices in most entries, a result in the study of matrix\n291\trigidity [Val77]. Related interpolation gives rigidity bounds for Kronecker powers in [AGP23,\n292\tTheorem 6.1]. Dvir and Liu [DL20, Claim 2.20 and Lemmas 3.3–3.4] use a spectral approach: sparse\n293\tchanges to a function invariant under permutations of its coordinates make many Fourier coefficients\n294\tvanish, reducing the rank of its group-circulant matrix.\n295\t   Our construction also interpolates (−1)j on a short interval, now to obtain a polynomial in\n296\tcommuting permutation matrices. The same polynomial gives both the rank bound for I − M0\n297\tand the restrictions on the nonzero entries of M0 . We prove these properties and their quantitative\n298\tbounds directly in Section 4.\n299\t1.1.6. Sparsification and parity tests. We use the counting-preserving version [DHM+ 14, Appendix A]\n300\tof the sparsification lemma [IPZ01], with quantitative bounds from [CIP06, Section 6] and [Cal09,\n301\tLemma 5.14 and equation (5.4)]. This partitions the satisfying assignments among sparse formulas\n302\ton the same Boolean variables. Appendix A records the disjoint branching argument and the bounds\n303\tneeded when its parameter varies with the input size.\n304\t   The initial parity tests are the constraints of a Tanner code [Tan81, Section II], applied to the\n305\tbinary vector recording which clauses an assignment violates. We use the edge-based expander-code\n306\tconstruction of [SS96, Sections IV and VI]: each graph edge carries one bit, and each vertex requires\n307\tits incident bits to belong to a fixed binary linear code. The local code’s minimum distance, together\n308\twith graph expansion, forces every nonzero vector satisfying all these constraints to have large\n309\tsupport. This construction and distance argument are standard. Lemma 6.1 supplies the parameter\n310\tchoices needed here, including the bounds on the number and support size of the tests, and constructs\n311\tthe graph and local code by deterministic exhaustive searches. The graph is the bipartite double\n312\t\f                               THE EXPONENTIAL TIME HYPOTHESIS IS FALSE                                          7\n313\t\n314\t\n315\tcover of a Cayley multigraph. The shifts defining that multigraph nearly balance every nonconstant\n316\tparity on the base group: this is the small-bias condition [NN93, AR94], stated explicitly in (6.2).\n317\tFixing unused edge bits to zero gives a code on exactly m coordinates. Additional parity tests\n318\texclude the remaining nonzero violation vectors. We choose these tests by the averaging principle\n319\tunderlying the method of conditional expectations [Rag88], using exact counts to evaluate each\n320\tcandidate. Section 6 gives the constructions and quantitative bounds needed here.\n321\t   Theorem 1.1 invites a renewed search for algorithms for problems whose known hardness results\n322\trelied on ETH or SETH. The examples in Section 1.1.3 suggest both immediate consequences and\n323\tproblems for which new algorithmic ideas are needed.\n324\t\n325\t1.2. Notation. For an integer s ⩾ 0, write [s] = {1, . . . , s}, with [0] = ∅. For a condition E,\n326\tthe scalar 1{E} equals one if E holds and zero otherwise. We use natural logarithms, writing\n327\tlog2 when the base is two. For complexity bounds, poly(t1 , . . . , ts ) denotes a bound of the form\n328\t(1+t1 +· · ·+ts )O(1) , with absolute implicit constants. Subscripts, as in polyk,c (t1 , . . . , ts ), allow those\n329\tconstants to depend on the indicated fixed parameters. Integers are stored in signed binary notation,\n330\tso an integer z uses O(log(|z| + 2)) bits. Rational numbers are stored as an integer numerator and a\n331\tpositive integer denominator; stated bit-length bounds apply to each of them. Space is measured in\n332\tbits. Arithmetic-operation counts are distinguished from bit-operation counts.\n333\t   For a finite set S, vectors in QS have coordinates indexed by S and are column vectors unless\n334\tindicated otherwise. For U ⊆ S, the indicator vector 1U ∈ {0, 1}S has entries (1U )v = 1{v∈U } for\n335\tv ∈ S. We write ⃗0 for the zero vector. For v ∈ S, the row δv ∈ Q1×S selects coordinate v, so\n336\tδv x = xv . A vector indexed by U ⊆ S is extended to S by putting zero in each coordinate of S \\ U .\n337\t   We write X ⃗ = (X1 , . . . , Xn ) for the vector of formal variables and use arrows for polynomial\n338\tcoefficient vectors such as P⃗f,v (T ). For a coefficient row p⃗ = (pi )i∈S and a vector z = (zi )i∈S , we\n339\t                                 p, z⟩ = i∈S pi zi . In particular, ⟨P⃗f,v (T ), ⃗\n340\t                                         P\n341\twrite their scalar pairing as ⟨⃗                                                X⟩ is a scalar linear form in\n342\tthe variables Xi .\n343\t   For a polynomial P (X,  ⃗ z) in the variables X  ⃗ and a variable z, [z j ]P denotes the coefficient of\n344\tz , viewed as a polynomial in ⃗\n345\t j                                  X, and degX⃗ P denotes total degree in ⃗     X. The degree of a row of\n346\tpolynomials is the largest degree of any entry. Unless otherwise specified, polynomial coefficients are\n347\trational and matrix kernels are taken over Q.\n348\t\n349\t\n350\t                                          2. Technique overview"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01NBba36F1ZDP5St8tNuyaPN","agent_id":"ada7cdc6671aeaeca","session_id":"9be64548-7b53-4f99-81aa-4ed97f3b0de3","uuid":"092d4281-bd14-4822-b424-a2a2b3284a71","timestamp":"2026-11-04T18:13:07.302Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01U4MFJzZmTcyWSRbbEMHHh9","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Second opinion on counting method"}
