2k max(D0 , P )\nmonomials of degree at most k.\nProof. At a stage with current matrix A, enumerate the ordered lists\n                          γ = ((e1 , c1 ), . . . , (eP , cP )) ∈ C := ([m] × {0, 1})P .\nThe list γ represents the binary row hγ with\n                                       \u0010 X \u0011\n                              (hγ )e =       cj mod 2               (e ∈ [m]).\n                                            j:ej =e\n\nThere are (2m)P lists; distinct lists are still counted separately when they represent the same row.\nWrite A; hγ for the matrix obtained by appending hγ to A.\n  Fix a nonsatisfying assignment x accepted by A. The initial rows remain in A, so | supp v(x)| ⩾\nm/P . For a uniform list γ, the probability that none of e1 , . . . , eP belongs to this support is at most\n                                         (1 − 1/P )P ⩽ e−1 < 1/2.\nConditional on the indices hitting the support, choose one hit position j. After fixing every coefficient\nexcept cj , the uniform bit cj makes hγ · v(x) uniform modulo 2. This remains true when some clause\nindices are repeated. Thus, for each such x, at least a quarter of the candidate lists give a row that\nrejects x.\n   Summing this bound over the nonsatisfying assignments still passing, we find\n                                          Eγ D(A; hγ ) ⩽ 34 D(A).\nFor every candidate, compute N (A; hγ ) by counting the common zeros of its polynomial system.\nAppend a candidate attaining the minimum, breaking ties by enumeration order. Since N (A; hγ ) =\n#F + D(A; hγ ) for every γ, the chosen row leaves at most 34 D(A) nonsatisfying assignments. After\n3n rounds at most (3/4)3n 2n = (27/32)n < 1 remain; their number is an integer, so it is zero. The\nprocedure runs for a fixed number of rounds and never needs to know D(A) or #F .\n  The initial matrix has at most 3n rows, and every candidate is evaluated with at most 3n additional\nrows. A row listing s clauses expands, by (6.1), to at most 2k s mon