{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MjLrYRgEh3aye5TfKChDpg","type":"tool_result","content":"                     Appendix D. A sharper bound for exact counting\n   We prove Theorem 1.1 by contracting Boolean tensor networks. The two running times reflect the\nnumber of wires in the networks. A formula with m ⩽ Dn clauses gives a network with Ok,D (n)\nwires. For arbitrary formulas, we use the parity tests of Section 6. The degree bound of Lemma 5.2\nlets us index a monomial retained in the product of their polynomial lifts with Ok (n log log n) wires.\nWe give both encodings before proving the contraction theorem they use.\nD.1. Boolean tensor contraction. A Boolean tensor network is a bipartite multigraph G in which\neach edge carries a single bit. Each vertex v has an ordered list δ(v) of incident edges and a tensor\nTv assigning a ring element to each Boolean assignment to those edges. Parallel edges have distinct\nports. The contraction of the network is\n                                           X       Y\n                                  Z=                    Tv (x|δ(v) ).\n                                        x∈{0,1}E(G) v∈V (G)\n\nAn isolated vertex contributes its scalar entry. We measure the size of the network by its number of\nedges.\nTheorem D.1 (Boolean tensor contraction). Fix positive constants C, A, b. Let s ⩾ 2 be an integer,\nand let L be the total length of the graph and tensor descriptions of a bipartite tensor network with at\nmost Cs edges and maximum degree at most ⌈s9/10 ⌉. Suppose that the number of vertices is bounded\nby a fixed polynomial in s, and that the entries belong to\n                           BP0 ,ℓ = (Z/2P0 Z)[V ]/(V ℓ ),     1 ⩽ P0 , ℓ ⩽ Cs.\nSuppose, uniformly for every vertex and Boolean choice of its ports, that its entry can be computed\n                                          9/10\nfrom the description in time (s + L)b 2As      and space (s + L)b . Then the contraction can be