                  H(T ) =                                                          (3.3)\n                                    x∈{0,1}n f ∈F\n\nThe replacements restore the inputs at zero and multiply the degree by at most R. Thus H(0) = Z\nand degT H ⩽ dH := dR. Our application will have d = O(n), as in the bounded-occurrence\nexample.\n3.2. The required rows, partitions, and measurements. We use the same design as in\nSection 2.4, with a group assignment for each factor in place of each clause. The final condition\nbelow records the coefficient bound needed for exact arithmetic. Here ξe denotes extension by zero\nfrom a group to all n coordinates.\nDefinition 3.1. For the factor scopes (If )f ∈F , let r ⩾ 2, D ⩾ 1, and 1 ⩽ R < Q be integers, and\nlet 0 < τ ⩽ 1/2. We require the following data and properties.\n    (1) For each t ∈ [Q], a partition [n] = St,1 ⊔ · · · ⊔ St,r , allowing empty parts, with |St,g | ⩽ 2n/r.\n    (2) For every factor f ∈ F, a sequence σf ∈ [r]Q . The value σf (t) assigns factor f to a group at\n        point t.\n\f22                                                      OPENAI\n\n\n     (3) For every t ∈ [Q] and g ∈ [r], an integer matrix Ct,g ∈ Z[ct,g ]×St,g , where ct,g is a nonnegative\n         integer. Each row has sum of absolute values at most D, and\n                                             X r\n                                                 ct,g ⩽ 2τ n.\n                                                  g=1\n\n         For an assignment x, the entries of Ct,g (x|St,g ) are the measurements on group g.\n     (4) For every f ∈ F and v ∈ If , a row P⃗f,v (T ) ∈ Q[T ]1×n with P⃗f,v (0) = δv and deg P⃗f,v ⩽ R.\n         For every t ∈ [Q], g ∈ [r] \\ {σf (t)}, and ξ ∈ QSt,g , require\n                                    Ct,g ξ = ⃗0     =⇒          ⟨P⃗f,v (t), ξ⟩\n                                                                            e = 0,\n\n         where ξe ∈ Qn is the extension of ξ by zero.\n     (5) With dden = (R − 1)!Q!