ded by zero outside Sh . For any E ⊆ [n], the sum\n                                                        X        r\n                                                                 Y\n                                         ZE =                           Ag (x)\n                                                   x∈{0,1}n      g=1\n                                                  xi =1 (i∈E)\n\ncan be evaluated by examining at most\n                                                                r\n                                                                X\n                                            2(2D + 1)       c\n                                                                      2|Sg |\n                                                                g=1\n\nBoolean vectors, with at most one Ag evaluation, poly(n, r, c, log(D + 2)) additional bit operations,\nand O(1) ring operations per vector. Apart from the evaluators’ own storage and O(1) ring elements,\nthe procedure uses poly(n, r, c, log(D + 2)) bits of space.\n\f                               THE EXPONENTIAL TIME HYPOTHESIS IS FALSE                                                  23\n\n\nProof. For yg ∈ Zcg , define\n                                                                                r\n                                                                                Y\n                    Xg (yg ) = {u ∈ {0, 1}Sg : Cg u = yg },             Y=          {−D, . . . , D}cg .\n                                                                                g=1\n\nEvery tuple of measurements of a Boolean assignment belongs to Y, and |Y| = (2D + 1)c . We first\nidentify the contribution of a fixed measurement tuple ⃗y = (y1 , . . . , yr ). If every Xg (yg ) is nonempty,\nlet x̄g ∈ Xg (yg ) be a representative for each g, and put\n                           Xg,E (yg ) = {u ∈ Xg (yg ) : ui = 1 for all i ∈ E ∩ Sg }.\nIf xh ∈ Xh (yh ) for every h, then xh − x̄h ∈ ker Ch . Applying the invar