{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgiN7XqWM9ZBaPJv7avFQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 01 is done and the gate passes. Nothing was committed, nothing written under `solutions/`, and nothing in the untrusted clone was built or run.\n\n**What was found**\n\n- **No subexponential exact pathwidth algorithm is on record.** The best bases are 1.89 (Kitsunai et al. 2016, held) and 1.9657 (Suchan & Villanger 2009, held). Cutwidth, linear arrangement and feedback arc set are still at `O*(2^n)` with no base below 2, which Bentert et al. (ITCS 2025, held) state explicitly. Treewidth and bandwidth bases are recorded, two of them flagged \"from memory, not held\".\n- **One ETH-conditional bound on computing a width exists, and it is linear.** Bonnet (JACM 2025, abstract on disk, paper not held) proves exact treewidth needs `2^{Ω(n)}` under ETH by a self-contained reduction from 3-SAT, and says the older chains through cutwidth or pathwidth on bounded-degree graphs gave only `2^{Ω(√n)}`. No sentence \"no `2^{o(n)}` for pathwidth unless ETH fails\" was found in any held paper or on-disk title, and no SETH bound on computing any width.\n- **The counting method's real budget is total incidence, not clause width.** The paper computes a sum over a Boolean cube of a product of local factors. In the tensor form the exponent is in the number of wires, which is the total variable–constraint incidence, and a wide threshold constraint is allowed as one vertex of degree up to `s^{9/10}`. Fixed `k` is needed only for sparsification and the replacement-row degree. For pathwidth, a certificate of width at most `k` needs `o(n√log n)` wires, or `o(n√log n/(log log n)²)` CNF variables on the verified route. The position and nested-chain encodings are `Θ(n²)`, and any witness that determines the layout is at least `n log n` bits, so items 04, 05 and 09 must look for a different certificate object.\n- **The formally verified statement carries the weaker exponent** `n(log log n)²/√log n`. The sharper bounds of Theorem 1.1 are paper-only. Item 02 must restate the verified form.\n- **Kill check: not killed** on either criterion. The second criterion asks for a demonstration that the method cannot apply to any NP-hard width problem, and none exists in either direction.\n\n**What was written**\n\n- `reports/subexp_pathwidth.md` §1, with §0 reserved for item 11. The section ends with \"what this does not claim\" and regenerate commands.\n- `reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/01_audit/`: README with regenerate commands, the subagent's blind second reading (which agreed on every point), and the text extracts, which are git-ignored.\n- PROGRESS.md iteration section with the two findings that redirect items 03 and 04/05, and the missing papers for item 11. `iterations.md` marks item 01 done. Current count is 1 of 12.\n\n**Gate**: lake build ok, zero sorries, 1,931 tests passed plus 123 in the pathwidth solver, paper builds with no undefined references.\n\nOne subagent of the allowed four was used."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1116,"cache_read_input_tokens":273045,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1116,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":767,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9be64548-7b53-4f99-81aa-4ed97f3b0de3","uuid":"03bf1ba1-66ab-409f-a75f-97308d340fbe","timestamp":"2026-11-04T18:31:20.777Z","request_id":"req_011CgiN7XQxw6CZCTEfTs4Hf"}
